{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 157, "title": "Thumb arthritis", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Thumb Arthritis\n\nAbstract\n\nThis case study details the assessment and treatment of a 47-year-old male presenting with chronic right thumb pain, stiffness, and decreased grip strength. A diagnosis of osteoarthritis of the thumb carpometacarpal (CMC) joint was established based on clinical examination and radiographic findings. A tailored physiotherapy program incorporating strengthening, stretching, joint mobilizations, and patient education was implemented, resulting in significant improvement in pain, function, and overall quality of life.\n\nCase Presentation\n\nA 47-year-old male construction worker presents with a 2-year history of right thumb pain, stiffness, and decreased grip strength. The patient reports constant pain, especially with gripping and pinching activities. He also experiences morning stiffness and decreased range of motion, particularly with thumb flexion and abduction. He reports an insidious onset of symptoms and has not sought formal medical treatment. The patient reports that his pain is worse in the morning and after prolonged use. He denies any history of trauma or injury to the right thumb.\n\nClinical Examination\n\nSubjective\n\nHistory\n\nPatient reports pain and stiffness in the right thumb, particularly with gripping and pinching. He also reports decreased grip strength. Patient reports morning stiffness and decreased range of motion. Patient reports pain is worse in the morning and after prolonged use. Patient denies history of trauma or injury to the right thumb.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nInspection\n\nRight thumb appears swollen and deformed.\n\nPalpation\n\nTenderness noted over the right thumb CMC joint.\n\nRange of Motion\n\nRight thumb flexion and abduction are limited.\n\nStrength\n\nDecreased right thumb grip strength.\n\nSpecial Tests\n\nPositive grind test.\n\nRadiographic Findings\n\nX-rays of the right thumb CMC joint reveal degenerative changes with joint space narrowing, osteophyte formation, and subchondral sclerosis.\n\nDiagnosis\n\nOsteoarthritis of the right thumb CMC joint.\n\nTreatment\n\nPatient was instructed to rest and avoid activities that exacerbate pain. Patient was advised to apply ice packs to the affected area. Patient was also advised to use a thumb spica cast.\n\nTreatment Plan\n\n1. Physical Therapy\n\n2. Pain Management\n\n3. Education\n\nOutcome\n\nPatient reported significant improvement in pain and decreased stiffness. Patient reported increased range of motion and increased grip strength. Patient reported increased function. Patient reported increased quality of life.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe patient's symptoms are consistent with osteoarthritis of the thumb CMC joint. The patient was treated with physical therapy. The physical therapy program included strengthening, stretching, joint mobilizations, and patient education. Physical therapy was effective in improving pain, stiffness, range of motion, and function.\n\nConclusion\n\nPhysical therapy is an effective treatment for osteoarthritis of the thumb CMC joint. The physical therapy program included strengthening, stretching, joint mobilizations, and patient education. The patient reported significant improvement in pain, stiffness, range of motion, and function.\n\nOutcome\n\nPatient reported significant improvement in pain and decreased stiffness. Patient reported increased range of motion and increased grip strength. Patient reported increased function. Patient reported increased quality of life.\n\nFinal Notes\n\nThis case study describes the successful treatment of thumb arthritis.", "rouge1": 0.37577255871446236, "rouge2": 0.047087980173482036, "rougeL": 0.17552533992583436, "bert_precision": 0.7745909690856934, "bert_recall": 0.7830467224121094, "bert_f1": 0.7787958979606628, "gen_words": 485, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_ac