{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 165, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Case Study: A 5-Year-Old Boy with Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot)\n\n**Patient:** 5-year-old male\n\n**Presenting Complaint:** Parents brought their 5-year-old son to the physiotherapy department with a history of congenital talipes equinovarus (clubfoot). The parents noted that his left foot was significantly turned inwards, upwards, and downwards, making it difficult for him to walk properly. They also reported that the foot was rigid and stiff, and that he was often in a \"rocker-bottom\" position. He has been diagnosed with clubfoot by a pediatrician and was previously treated with casting.\n\n**History:**\n\n* **Birth History:** Full-term infant, uncomplicated vaginal delivery. No known risk factors for clubfoot.\n* **Developmental History:** Mother reported that the baby's feet were noticed to be turned inwards at birth, and they were assessed by a pediatrician who confirmed the diagnosis of clubfoot.\n* **Previous Treatment:** The infant was treated with serial casting from birth to 6 months of age. A posterior plaster cast was applied to the foot and leg, which was changed every 2 weeks. After casting, the foot was gently dorsiflexed and abducted.\n* **Current Status:** The infant was then treated with serial casting from 6 months to 12 months of age. A posterior plaster cast was applied to the foot and leg, which was changed every 2 weeks. After casting, the foot was gently dorsiflexed and abducted.\n* **Current Complaint:** The infant was then treated with serial casting from 6 months to 12 months of age. A posterior plaster cast was applied to the foot and leg, which was changed every 2 weeks. After casting, the foot was gently dorsiflexed and abducted. The infant is now 5 years old and still has a rigid, stiff left foot in a rocker-bottom position. He has difficulty walking and is often in a limp. He is referred to physiotherapy for further treatment.\n\n**Examination:**\n\n* **Subjective:** The child was cooperative and well-presented. He was able to walk with a limp, favoring his right leg. His left foot was in a rocker-bottom position with the heel elevated and the toes pointing downwards. He was able to dorsiflex his left foot to a slight degree, but it was difficult to fully dorsiflex it. He was able to abduct his left foot to a slight degree, but it was difficult to fully abduct it. He was able to plantarflex his left foot to a slight degree, but it was difficult to fully plantarflex it. He reported pain in his left foot when it was dorsiflexed.\n\n* **Objective:**\n    * **Range of Motion (ROM):**\n        * **Ankle Dorsiflexion:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n        * **Ankle Plantarflexion:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n        * **Foot Abduction:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n        * **Foot Adduction:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n        * **Hip ROM:** Normal\n        * **Knee ROM:** Normal\n        * **Foot ROM:**\n            * **Plantarflexion:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n            * **Dorsiflexion:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n            * **Abduction:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n            * **Adduction:** 10 degrees (normal is 15-30 degrees)\n        * **Gait Analysis:**\n            * **Walking Pattern:** Antalgic gait with a limp on the right leg.\n            * **Foot Position:** Rocker-bottom position on the left foot.\n            * **Muscle Strength:**\n                * **Tibialis Anterior:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Gastrocnemius:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Soleus:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Tibialis Posterior:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Peroneals:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Gluteus Medius:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Hamstrings:** 5/5 (normal)\n                * **Quadriceps:** 5/5 (normal)\n                * **Hip Flexors:** 5/5 (normal)\n                * **Calves:** 5/5 (normal)\n                * **Ankle Dorsiflexors:** 4/5 (weak)\n                * **Ankle Plant", "rouge1": 0.344689378757515, "rouge2": 0.05220883534136546, "rougeL": 0.14829659318637275, "bert_precision": 0.7566660642623901, "bert_recall": 0.7489182949066162, "bert_f1": 0.7527722716331482, "gen_words": 578, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.1, "judge_overall": 0.12, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "This case study has significant issues. The history section is repetitive and contradictory - the same casting treatment is described three times with copy-paste errors. The patient is described as a 5-year-old but the history keeps referring to 'infant.' The 'rocker-bottom' description is inconsistent with classic clubfoot (which presents with equinus/varus, not rocker-bottom - that's a flatfoot deformity). The gait analysis contradicts itself (limp on right leg in one place, left foot pathology elsewhere). ROM values are identical across all planes (all 10 degrees) which is clinically implau", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2424, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 179, "title": "TMJ Jaw and Ear Pain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: TMJ Jaw and Ear Pain\n\nPatient History\n\nPatient Name: John Doe\n\nAge: 42\n\nOccupation: Office worker\n\nPresenting Complaint: John Doe is a 42-year-old office worker who presents to physiotherapy with a 3-month history of intermittent right-sided jaw pain, which he describes as a dull ache that worsens with chewing, talking, and yawning. He also reports intermittent right-sided ear pain, which he describes as a sharp, stabbing pain that sometimes radiates into his neck and shoulder. He also reports some stiffness in his right jaw when opening his mouth wide and difficulty chewing hard foods. He also reports some tinnitus in his right ear.\n\nPast Medical History: John Doe has no significant past medical history. He is a non-smoker and does not drink alcohol. He has no known allergies.\n\nSocial History: John Doe is married with two children. He works a sedentary office job. He reports that he is under a lot of stress at work. He reports that he sleeps 6-7 hours per night and that he has been experiencing some difficulty falling asleep in the last month.\n\nPhysical Examination Findings\n\n1.  TMJ Examination:\n    *   Range of Motion: Limited opening of the right jaw to 30 mm bilaterally.\n    *   Palpation: Tenderness to palpation over the right temporomandibular joint (TMJ) and surrounding muscles.\n    *   Muscle Testing: Mild tenderness in the masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid muscles bilaterally.\n    *   Muscle Strength: Normal muscle strength bilaterally.\n    *   Jaw Clenching: Patient reports that he clenches his jaw subconsciously during the day.\n    *   Jaw Deviation: No deviation of the jaw during opening.\n\n2.  Ear Examination:\n    *   Otoscopy: Normal otoscopy.\n    *   Hearing Test: Normal hearing test.\n    *   Tinnitus: Patient reports subjective tinnitus in the right ear.\n\n3.  Neurological Examination:\n    *   Motor: Normal motor strength bilaterally.\n    *   Sensory: Normal sensory sensation bilaterally.\n    *   Reflexes: Normal reflexes bilaterally.\n\n4.  Musculoskeletal Examination:\n    *   Neck: Mild stiffness in the right cervical spine.\n    *   Shoulder: Mild tenderness in the right shoulder.\n\nAssessment\n\nBased on the patient's history and physical examination findings, the patient has signs and symptoms consistent with temporomandibular disorder (TMD), which can include TMJ dysfunction, myofascial pain, and associated symptoms such as ear pain, tinnitus, and headache. The patient's stress level may be a contributing factor to his symptoms.\n\nIntervention\n\nTreatment Plan\n\n1.  Patient Education: Educated the patient about TMD, its causes, and symptoms. Educated the patient about stress management techniques, including relaxation exercises and deep breathing. Educated the patient about proper posture and ergonomics at his desk.\n\n2.  Soft Tissue Mobilization: Performed soft tissue mobilization to the muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid) and the cervical spine.\n\n3.  Joint Mobilization: Performed gentle joint mobilization to the right TMJ.\n\n4.  Activity Modification: Advised the patient to avoid chewing hard foods and to take frequent short breaks from chewing during the day. Advised the patient to avoid excessive talking and yawning.\n\n5.  Stretching Exercises: Performed stretching exercises for the muscles of mastication and the cervical spine.\n\n6.  Heat/Cold Therapy: Performed heat therapy on the muscles of mastication and cold therapy on the neck muscles.\n\n7.  Electrotherapy: Performed electrotherapy on the muscles of mastication and cervical spine.\n\n8.  Taping: Performed Kinesio taping on the muscles of mastication and cervical spine.\n\nOutcome\n\nAfter 8 weeks of physiotherapy treatment, the patient reported a significant improvement in his right-sided jaw pain and ear pain. He reported that his jaw opening has increased to 40 mm bilaterally. He reported that his chewing has improved and that he is able to chew harder foods without pain. He reported that his tinnitus has decreased. He reported that his neck stiffness has decreased and that his sleep has improved. He reported that he is able to manage his stress better. He reported that he was able to return to work full time.\n\nConclusion\n\nThe patient's symptoms of TMJ jaw and ear pain improved significantly with physiotherapy treatment. The treatment included patient education, soft tissue mobilization, joint mobilization, activity modification, stretching exercises, heat/cold therapy, electrotherapy, and taping. The patient was able to return to work full time after 8 weeks of physiotherapy treatment.", "rouge1": 0.45569620253164556, "rouge2": 0.098256735340729, "rougeL": 0.16930379746835444, "bert_precision": 0.7662312984466553, "bert_recall": 0.7784204483032227, "bert_f1": 0.7722777724266052, "gen_words": 677, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.52, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for TMD with appropriate history, examination, and treatment. Clinical coherence is reasonable - history flows logically to assessment. However, diagnostic accuracy lacks special tests (e.g., load test, provocation tests), no differential diagnosis listed (e.g., otitis media, trigeminal neuralgia, dental pathology), and no imaging consideration. Treatment completeness is poor - no dosage, frequency, intensity, or session numbers specified for any intervention; no functional progression outlined. Patient safety is adequate but red flags (e.g", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4934, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 184, "title": "Knee injury", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Anterior Knee Pain & Patellofemoral Dysfunction\n\n**Patient:** Mr. John Smith, 48 years old\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\n\nMr. Smith is a self-employed tradesman (carpenter) who has been experiencing pain in his right knee for the past 6 months. The pain is located anteriorly, primarily around the patella, and often worsens after prolonged standing or walking, especially on uneven surfaces. He also experiences occasional clicking and a sensation of \"catching\" in the knee. He has noticed a gradual increase in the time it takes to perform his daily tasks, including climbing stairs and carrying materials. He has tried resting the knee, ice, and over-the-counter anti-inflammatory medication with limited success. He has no history of previous knee injuries.\n\n**Subjective Examination:**\n\nMr. Smith reports the pain is worse when walking up and down stairs, climbing onto vehicles, and walking on uneven surfaces. He describes the pain as a dull ache that sometimes progresses to sharp pain. He reports that the knee feels \"stiff\" in the morning and after prolonged sitting. He reports that his patella sometimes feels like it is \"slipping\" out to the side. He reports that his knee sometimes feels \"tight\" and \"locked\" and occasionally experiences a \"clicking\" sensation. He describes a feeling of \"weakness\" around his knee. He reports that he has been sleeping poorly due to the pain.\n\n**Objective Examination:**\n\n*   **Inspection:** Normal gait, no obvious swelling or deformity.\n*   **Palpation:** Tenderness is present over the medial and lateral retinaculum, particularly over the patellar tendon insertion. Tenderness is also present over the medial and lateral aspects of the patella.\n*   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Active and passive knee flexion is reduced by 10 degrees compared to the left knee. There is no significant limitation in extension.\n*   **Muscle Strength:** Hip abductors, quadriceps, and hamstrings are tested. Quadriceps strength is 7/10 bilaterally. Hip abductor strength is 7/10 bilaterally. Hamstring strength is 8/10 bilaterally.\n*   **Special Tests:**\n    *   **Patellar Tap Test:** Positive for patellar apprehension.\n    *   **Patellar Grind Test:** Positive for patellar tenderness.\n    *   **Patellar Compression Test:** Positive for medial and lateral patellar compression.\n    *   **Ober's Test:** Positive for patellar hypermobility.\n    *   **Hop Test:** Positive for patellar instability.\n    *   **Single Leg Balance Test:** Positive for quadriceps weakness.\n    *   **Standing Long Jump Test:** Positive for quadriceps weakness.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\nBased on the patient's history, subjective and objective examination findings, the most likely diagnosis is Anterior Knee Pain, likely secondary to Patellofemoral Dysfunction (PFD) and associated quadriceps weakness and patellar instability.\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\n**Phase 1: Initial Management (Weeks 1-4)**\n\n*   **Modalities:** Ice packs applied to the anterior knee for 15 minutes 3 times per day. Heat packs applied to the posterior knee for 15 minutes 3 times per day.\n*   **Exercise:**\n    *   **Quadriceps Strengthening:** Isometrics (quadriceps contraction against resistance) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Hamstring Stretching:** Hamstring stretch (lying on back, pulling heel towards glute) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Gluteal Strengthening:** Gluteus medius abduction exercise (standing side-lying abduction) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Patellar Mobilization:** Patellar glide exercises (medial and lateral) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Hip Abduction:** Standing hip abduction 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n*   **Education:** Patient educated on activity modification, importance of proper footwear, and home exercise program.\n\n**Phase 2: Progressive Strengthening & Stretching (Weeks 5-8)**\n\n*   **Modalities:** Continue ice and heat as needed.\n*   **Exercise:**\n    *   **Quadriceps Strengthening:** Mini-squats (bodyweight) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Hamstring Stretching:** Hamstring stretch (seated, leaning against a table) 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3 times per day.\n    *   **Gluteal Strengthening:** Clamshell exercise 3 sets of 10 repetitions, 3", "rouge1": 0.2676443629697525, "rouge2": 0.029384756657483933, "rougeL": 0.11915673693858847, "bert_precision": 0.7334818840026855, "bert_recall": 0.7612499594688416, "bert_f1": 0.747107982635498, "gen_words": 616, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.35, "judge_overall": 0.42, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Simultaneous prescription of ice to anterior knee AND heat to posterior knee 3x/day is contradictory and clinically inappropriate. Patellar mobilization without specifying direction relative to instability findings could exacerbate patellar instability. Non-standard muscle strength grading (7/10) co", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a reasonable clinical narrative for patellofemoral pain syndrome in a middle-aged tradesman. Clinical coherence is generally logical but has some inconsistencies. Diagnostic accuracy has issues: Ober's test assesses IT band tightness, not patellar hypermobility; muscle strength grading uses a non-standard 10-point scale instead of MRC 0-5; the 'Hop Test' and 'Standing Long Jump' are functional tests not specific patellar instability tests. Treatment plan includes simultaneous ice AND heat application which is contradictory and potentially inappropriate. Patellar mobiliz", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4308, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 193, "title": "Spinal Cord Injury", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Spinal Cord Injury\n\nAbstract\n\nSpinal cord injury (SCI) is a devastating condition that can result in long-term disability and loss of function. This case study outlines the management and outcomes of a patient with a thoracic spinal cord injury.\n\nPatient History\n\nA 35-year-old male presented with acute onset paraplegia following a motor vehicle accident. The patient sustained a T11-T12 fracture resulting in complete loss of sensation and motor function below the level of injury. The patient had a T12-L1 fusion performed and was discharged home with a tracheostomy and ventilator. The patient has been in a wheelchair for the past 6 months. The patient has had multiple surgeries and skin breakdown.\n\nExamination\n\nPhysical examination revealed decreased sensation and motor function below the level of injury. The patient is able to move his upper extremities and trunk. The patient has no bowel or bladder function. The patient has a tracheostomy and ventilator.\n\nTreatment\n\nThe patient underwent a course of physiotherapy to improve mobility and prevent complications. The patient was started on a course of physical therapy including range of motion exercises, strengthening exercises, and gait training. The patient was also started on a course of occupational therapy to improve function and independence. The patient was also started on a course of speech therapy to improve communication.\n\nOutcome\n\nThe patient was able to return home to his family. The patient was able to return to work. The patient was able to return to his community. The patient was able to return to his home. The patient was able to return to his community. The patient was able to return to his home. The patient was able to return to his community. The patient was able to return to his home. The patient was able to return to his com