{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 331, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Clubfoot\n\nPatient: A 2-month-old infant named \"Leo\"\n\nHistory:\n\nLeo was born full-term with no other complications. His mother noticed his feet were \"turned in\" at birth. She was referred to a pediatric orthopaedic specialist who diagnosed a bilateral congenital clubfoot. He was placed in a Ponseti cast from birth to 6 months of age. After cast treatment, his feet were still slightly turned in. He was referred to a pediatric physiotherapist for continued management.\n\nExamination Findings:\n\n*   Range of Motion: Mild limitation in dorsiflexion, plantar flexion, inversion, and eversion.\n*   Muscle Strength: Mild weakness in gastrocnemius and soleus muscles.\n*   Symmetry: Feet are still slightly asymmetrical with the left foot being more turned in than the right.\n*   Gait: No significant gait abnormalities.\n*   Other: No significant hip or knee abnormalities.\n\nTreatment Plan:\n\n*   Passive Stretching: Gentle passive stretching of gastrocnemius and soleus muscles.\n*   Active Exercises:\n    *   Ankle dorsiflexion exercises using a ball or resistance band.\n    *   Ankle plantar flexion exercises using a ball or resistance band.\n    *   Inversion and eversion exercises using a ball or resistance band.\n*   Proprioception Training: Using balance boards and visual cues.\n*   Gait Training: Encouraging normal gait patterns.\n*   Orthotics: Referral to pediatric orthopaedic surgeon for custom orthotics.\n\nOutcome:\n\nLeo was treated with physiotherapy for 6 months. He showed significant improvement in his range of motion and symmetry. His feet were no longer turned in, and he was able to walk normally. He was discharged from physiotherapy with a plan for ongoing stretching and proprioception exercises.", "rouge1": 0.18862491000719941, "rouge2": 0.02595529920692141, "rougeL": 0.07775377969762419, "bert_precis