{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 332, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Clubfoot: A Physiotherapy Case Study\n\n**Subject:** 18-month-old male infant\n\n**Presenting Complaint:** Right foot has been stiff and bent inwards since birth. Parents were referred by the pediatrician.\n\n**History:**\n\n* **Birth History:** Full term, vaginal delivery, no complications.\n* **Early History:** No significant developmental delay. No other family history of clubfoot.\n* **Examination:**\n    * **Clinical:** Right foot was dorsiflexed, plantarflexed, inverted, and abducted. There was a tight Achilles tendon. The right calf muscle was smaller than the left. There was a bony prominence at the calcaneus.\n    * **Imaging:** X-rays confirmed a posterior calcaneal spur.\n* **Diagnosis:** Clubfoot (Congenital Talipes Equinovarus)\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\n* **Initial Treatment:**\n    * **Serial Casting:** The infant was casted in a Ponseti method. The cast was applied to the infant's right foot to correct the foot's deformity.\n    * **Physical Therapy:** After the cast was removed, the infant was given exercises to stretch the tight muscles and strengthen the calf muscles.\n    * **Stretching:** The infant was given exercises to stretch the tight muscles in the right calf and foot.\n    * **Strengthening:** The infant was given exercises to strengthen the calf muscles.\n    * **Range of Motion:** The infant was given exercises to increase the range of motion in the right foot and ankle.\n    * **Mobilization:** The infant was given exercises to mobilize the right foot and ankle.\n    * **Physical Therapy:** The infant was given exercises to improve the strength and flexibility of the right foot and ankle.\n\n**Outcome:**\n\nThe infant's right foot was corrected after the cast was removed. The infant's right foot was put into a walking cast. The infant's right foot was then put into a walking cast. The infant's right foo