{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 335, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease - A Musculoskeletal Physiotherapy Perspective\n\nPatient: Mr. A.B.\n\nHistory of Presenting Complaint: Mr. A.B. is an 82-year-old gentleman with a 2-year history of Parkinson's disease, diagnosed at age 80. He presents with increasing difficulty with balance, gait, and mobility. He reports worsening postural instability, particularly when transitioning between activities, and increasing fatigue, especially in the afternoon. He also reports worsening stiffness in his legs, making it difficult to get out of a chair and climb stairs. He denies significant falls, but reports feeling unsteady and prone to tripping when getting out of his car or walking across the room. He reports increased reliance on his walking stick for stability, but even that is becoming less effective. His wife, Mrs. A.B., reports that he has been sleeping less and has developed a tremor in his hands, particularly when agitated or stressed. He also reports increased constipation and difficulty concentrating. He denies any significant pain, but reports stiffness in his neck and shoulders.\n\nPast Medical History: Parkinson's disease (diagnosed 2 years ago), hypertension, hyperlipidemia, osteoarthritis of the hips and knees, osteoporosis. He is a former smoker (quit 10 years ago).\n\nMedications: Levodopa/Carbidopa, Sinemet, Metformin, Atorvastatin, Lisinopril, Alendronate.\n\nPhysical Examination:\n\nGeneral: Mr. A.B. is alert and oriented to person, place, and time. He is slightly cachectic and appears frail. He ambulates with a double-limb swing and a wide base of gait, utilizing a walking stick for support. He is slow to initiate movement. He demonstrates mild bradykinesia, particularly with fine motor tasks. He has a resting tremor in his right hand, which increases when agitated. He has mild rigidity in his right leg. He has mild hyporeflexia. He has mild bilateral knee and hip osteoarthritis. He has osteoporosis.\n\nNeurological: Normal cranial nerve examination. Mild dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing. Mild dysdiadochokinesia on finger-to-finger testing. Mild weakness in proximal muscles bilaterally. Normal gait parameters.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Mild bilateral knee and hip osteoarthritis. Mild rigidity in the right leg. Mild bilateral ankle plantarflexion contractures. Mild bilateral knee flexion contractures. Mild bilateral hip flexor contractures. Mild bilateral shoulder flexion contractures. Mild bilateral shoulder abduction contractures. Mild bilateral hip abduction contractures. Mild bilateral knee extension contractures. Mild bilateral hip extension contractures. Mild bilateral ankle dorsiflexion contractures.\n\nImaging: X-rays of the hips and knees show mild osteoarthritis. X-rays of the shoulders show mild osteoarthritis. X-rays of the spine show mild degenerative changes in the thoracic spine.\n\nAssessment: Mr. A.B. has Parkinson's disease, which has led to significant gait and balance dysfunction. He has bradykinesia, rigidity, and resting tremor. His gait is slow, wide-based, and unsteady. He has increased postural instability, particularly with transitions and when walking. He has increased stiffness in his legs and decreased ability to get up and down from a chair. He has bilateral knee and hip osteoarthritis, which is contributing to his stiffness and decreased mobility. He has mild bilateral ankle plantarflexion contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased ankle dorsiflexion and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral knee flexion contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased hip flexion and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral hip flexor contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased hip extension and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral shoulder flexion contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased shoulder abduction and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral shoulder abduction contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased shoulder flexion and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral hip abduction contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased hip flexion and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral knee extension contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased hip flexion and increased stiffness. He has mild bilateral ankle dorsiflexion contractures, which are likely secondary to decreased ankle plantarflexion and increased stiffness. He has decreased ankle dorsiflexion, which is likely secondary to decreased ankle plantarflexion and increased stiffness.\n\nIntervention:\n\n1.  General Exercise Program:\n    *   General strengthening exercises for all major muscle groups (legs, hips, shoulders, arms) 2-3 times per week.\n    *   Balance exercises (e.g., standing on one leg, tandem stance, heel-toe walking) 2-3 times per week.\n    *   Aerobic exercise (e.g., walking, stationary cycling) 3-5 times per week, starting with short durations and gradually increasing.\n    *   Flexibility exercises (stretching) 2-3 times per week.\n    ", "rouge1": 0.29723756906077353, "rouge2": 0.051991150442477874, "rougeL": 0.11933701657458562, "bert_precision": 0.7298747301101685, "bert_recall": 0.7194082140922546, "bert_f1": 0.7246037125587463, "gen_words": 698, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.25, "judge_overall": 0.25, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Levodopa/Carbidopa and Sinemet are listed as separate medications when they are the same drug (Sinemet IS Levodopa/Carbidopa), suggesting a potential double-dosing error. Metformin is prescribed without a documented diabetes diagnosis. No fall prevention protocol is specified despite osteoporosis an", "judge_reasoning": "The case study has several significant issues. Clinical coherence is partially present but undermined by contradictory findings (e.g., 'normal gait parameters' yet described as slow/wide-based/unsteady). The assessment section contains logically circular and contradictory contracture descriptions (e.g., 'ankle plantarflexion contractures secondary to decreased ankle dorsiflexion' AND 'ankle dorsiflexion contractures secondary to decreased ankle plantarflexion'). Diagnostic accuracy is poor - Metformin listed without diabetes diagnosis, Levodopa and Sinemet listed as separate medications (they ", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3319, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 336, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease\n\nIntroduction\n\nParkinson's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder that affects the motor system. It is characterized by tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability. The onset of Parkinson's disease is usually in the age group of 60 years. The disease is associated with the degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra. The primary goal of physiotherapy in Parkinson's disease is to improve motor function, reduce symptoms, enhance mobility, and maintain independence. The treatment plan for Parkinson's disease is tailored to the individual patient's needs and symptoms. Physiotherapy interventions are primarily focused on gait training, balance training, stretching exercises, strengthening exercises, and aerobic exercises.\n\nClinical Presentation\n\nA 65-year-old male patient, Mr. John Smith, presented to the physiotherapy department with a complaint of worsening gait and balance issues over the past 6 months. He reported difficulty walking in crowded spaces, frequent falls, and reduced ability to perform activities of daily living (ADLs) such as dressing and bathing. His history revealed that he was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease 3 years ago. He was prescribed levodopa, but his symptoms had been progressively worsening over time.\n\nExamination Findings\n\nOn examination, Mr. Smith demonstrated significant postural instability, characterized by frequent falls. His gait was characterized by reduced stride length, increased stride time, and shuffling gait. He exhibited rigidity in his trunk and limbs. His tremor was primarily at rest. His cognitive function was intact, but he showed difficulty with executive function. His strength was reduced, and his muscle tone was increased. His range of motion was decreased.\n\nIntervention\n\nThe physiotherapy intervention included the following components:\n\n1.  Gait Training: Mr. Smith was taught a variety of gait strategies, including the front-leg lift and the step-up gait. He was also instructed on how to use a walker to improve his balance and stability.\n\n2.  Balance Training: Mr. Smith was taught various balance exercises, such as standing on one leg, tandem walking, and heel-toe walking. He was also instructed on how to use a balance board to improve his proprioception and coordination.\n\n3.  Stretching Exercises: Mr. Smith was taught a variety of stretching exercises to improve his range of motion and reduce muscle stiffness.\n\n4.  Strengthening Exercises: Mr. Smith was taught a variety of strengthening exercises to improve his muscle strength and endurance.\n\n5.  Aerobic Exercises: Mr. Smith was taught a variety of aerobic exercises, such as walking, swimming, and cycling, to improve his cardiovascular fitness and endurance.\n\nOutcome\n\nMr. Smith's gait and balance improved significantly after 3 months of physiotherapy. He was able to walk independently without a walker. His fall frequency decreased. He also showed improvement in his ADLs. Mr. Smith's family reported that he was more active and confident.", "rouge1": 0.2536412078152753, "rouge2": 0.0796302879488091, "rougeL": 0.11296625222024867, "bert_precision": 0.7881942987442017, "bert_recall": 0.7743852138519287, "bert_f1": 0.7812287211418152, "gen_words": 447, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.35, "judge_overall": 0.42, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Single-leg standing and balance board exercises are prescribed for a patient with frequent falls and significant postural instability without mention of supervision, safety precautions, or progressive grading. This could increase fall risk if followed without appropriate clinical oversight.", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent narrative for Parkinson's disease with appropriate clinical features (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability). Clinical reasoning flows logically from presentation to intervention. However, several deficiencies exist: (1) No standardised outcome measures used (e.g., Berg Balance Scale, UPDRS, TUG test); (2) Treatment lacks dosage specifics - no frequency, sets, reps, intensity, or progression criteria; (3) No red flags or safety considerations mentioned (fall risk management, freezing of gait strategies like auditory/visual cueing are absent); (", "clinical_msk_score": 0.437, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 337, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease - A Physiotherapy Approach\n\n**Client Information:**\n\n* **Name:** Mr. R. S. (Patient Preferred)\n* **Age:** 72 years\n* **Gender:** Male\n* **Occupation:** Retired Accountant\n* **Presenting Complaint:** Mr. R. S. presented to the physiotherapy clinic with increasing gait instability, balance problems, and difficulty with daily activities such as rising from a chair and walking independently. He also reported increasing stiffness and bradykinesia (slowness of movement) in his limbs. He felt that his movements were becoming more hesitant and less coordinated. He reported that he was finding it increasingly difficult to keep up with his family and maintain his independence at home. He also reported increased fatigue and a feeling of heaviness in his legs.\n\n**History of Presenting Complaint:**\n\nMr. R. S. was diagnosed with Parkinson's disease 5 years ago. He had initially presented to his neurologist with symptoms of tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability. He had been prescribed levodopa and a beta-blocker for tremor control. He had attended physiotherapy initially 2 years ago for gait and balance training, but had stopped attending regularly due to lack of motivation and fatigue. He had also attended occupational therapy for home safety modifications.\n\n**Past Medical History:**\n\n* Hypertension (diagnosed 15 years ago)\n* Hyperlipidemia (diagnosed 10 years ago)\n* Osteoarthritis (knees and back)\n* History of a mild stroke 10 years ago (no residual deficits)\n* History of a heart attack 10 years ago (post-operative cardiac rehabilitation)\n* Former smoker (quit 20 years ago)\n* Moderate alcohol consumption\n\n**Social History:**\n\n* Lives alone in a 2-bedroom apartment with his wife, Mrs. S.\n* Retired accountant, spends time reading and gardening\n* His wife is his primary caregiver, but she works full-time and has her own health issues.\n* He has 2 adult children who live out of town and visit occasionally.\n* He has limited mobility and is unable to drive.\n* He uses a walker for mobility support.\n* He has difficulty with stairs and getting in and out of the car.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n\n* **Gait:** Mr. R. S. presented with a shuffling gait with reduced stride length, decreased arm swing, and increased trunk sway. His gait was unsteady and he frequently required his walker for support. He had difficulty initiating movement and showed decreased step length. His gait was characterized by a forward trunk lean.\n* **Balance:** He had difficulty with tandem stance and dynamic balance, frequently losing his balance and requiring the use of his walker. He had a positive Romberg test.\n* **Strength:** Mild weakness was noted in his lower extremities (hip flexors, quadriceps, hamstrings) and upper extremities (shoulder abductors, elbow flexors).\n* **Range of Motion:** Mild stiffness and decreased range of motion were noted in his hips, knees, ankles, shoulders, and elbows.\n* **Coordination:** Decreased coordination was noted in his upper extremities, particularly in fine motor tasks.\n* **Sensation:** Sensation was intact.\n* **Neurological:** Parkinsonian features were present including rigidity (cogwheel and lead-pipe), bradykinesia, and postural instability. There were no upper motor neuron signs.\n* **Cardiovascular:** Normal vital signs.\n* **Respiratory:** Normal respiratory effort.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\nMr. R. S. has Parkinson's disease with significant gait and balance impairment, lower limb weakness, and decreased upper limb strength and coordination. His symptoms are impacting his independence and ability to participate in daily activities. His current treatment plan includes levodopa and a beta-blocker, but he is not attending physiotherapy regularly. He has decreased mobility and is at high risk for falls.\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\n* **Goal:** Improve gait, balance, strength, and function to increase independence and reduce fall risk.\n* **Interventions:**\n    * **Gait Training:**\n        * **High-Frequency Treadmill Training (HFTT):** Mr. R. S. will undergo HFTT 3 times per week for 20 minutes per session. This will help improve gait speed, stride length, and balance.\n        * **Balance Training:** Mr. R. S. will participate in balance exercises including tandem stance, single leg stance, and dynamic balance exercises (e.g., walking on a line, obstacle courses) 2 times per week for 20 minutes per session.\n    * **Strength Training:** Mr. R. S. will undergo progressive resistance training 2 times per week for 20 minutes per session, focusing on lower limb and upper limb strength.\n    * **Functional Training:** Mr. R. S. will", "rouge1": 0.3241491085899514, "rouge2": 0.0640713706407137, "rougeL": 0.10858995137763372, "bert_precision": 0.7560321092605591, "bert_recall": 0.7656893730163574, "bert_f1": 0.7608300447463989, "gen_words": 698, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.5, "judge_overall": 0.55, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for Parkinson's disease with appropriate history, examination findings, and treatment planning. Clinical coherence is good - logical flow from complaint to assessment. Diagnostic accuracy is reasonable with appropriate Parkinsonian features documented (cogwheel/lead-pipe rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability), though special tests are limited (only Romberg mentioned; no UPDRS, TUG, Berg Balance Scale). Treatment plan includes dosage for some interventions but is truncated (functional training section incomplete). 'High-Frequency Trea", "clinical_msk_score": 0.5305, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 338, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease and Rehabilitation\n\nAbstract\n\nParkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder that primarily affects the motor system, leading to a range of symptoms including tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability. These symptoms can significantly impact mobility, balance, and quality of life. Physiotherapy plays a crucial role in managing PD by improving motor function, reducing fall risk, and enhancing overall well-being. This case study outlines the assessment, interventions, and outcomes of a 68-year-old female patient with PD who participated in a 12-week physiotherapy program.\n\nClient Profile\n\nPatient: Mrs. A. R.\n\nAge: 68\n\nGender: Female\n\nOccupation: Retired Teacher\n\nDiagnosis: Parkinson's Disease\n\nPresenting Complaint: Mrs. R. presented with increased difficulty walking, balance issues, and a general decline in functional independence. She reported experiencing frequent falls, especially in the morning, and difficulty with tasks such as dressing and bathing. Her family members were concerned about her safety and mobility.\n\nAssessment\n\nClinical Examination:\n\n*   Gait Analysis: Mrs. R. exhibited a shuffling gait with reduced stride length and increased cadence. Her gait was characterized by festination (accelerating steps) and a decreased base of support, making her prone to falls.\n\n*   Balance Assessment: Mrs. R. demonstrated impaired balance with reduced static and dynamic balance. She had difficulty maintaining a stable stance and required compensatory strategies such as leaning forward and using her arms for support.\n\n*   Strength and Range of Motion (ROM): Mild muscle weakness was noted in the lower limbs, particularly in the quadriceps and hip flexors. ROM in the hips, knees, and ankles was reduced, likely due to rigidity and bradykinesia.\n\n*   Neurological Examination: No focal neurological deficits were observed. However, she had bradykinesia and rigidity, consistent with her diagnosis.\n\n*   Functional Assessment: Mrs. R. had difficulty with activities of daily living (ADLs) such as dressing, bathing, and stair climbing. She required assistance with ambulation and transfer tasks.\n\nInterventions\n\nA 12-week physiotherapy program was designed to address Mrs. R.'s deficits. The program included:\n\n*   Balance Training: Exercises focusing on improving static and dynamic balance, such as tandem stance, weight shifting, and proprioceptive exercises.\n\n*   Gait Training: Exercises designed to improve gait speed, stride length, and cadence, such as high-intensity interval training (HIIT), rhythmic walking, and obstacle courses.\n\n*   Strengthening Exercises: Resistance training exercises targeting the lower limb muscles, such as leg press, hamstring curls, and calf raises.\n\n*   Flexibility and Stretching: Stretching exercises to improve ROM and reduce stiffness, particularly in the hips and ankles.\n\n*   Functional Training: Exercises simulating daily tasks such as stair climbing, transfers, and dressing.\n\n*   Aerobic Exercise: Low-impact aerobic activities such as walking on a treadmill and stationary cycling to improve cardiovascular fitness and endurance.\n\n*   Education: Education on PD management, fall prevention strategies, and energy conservation techniques.\n\nOutcome\n\nAfter 12 weeks of physiotherapy, Mrs. R. demonstrated significant improvements in her functional mobility and balance. Her gait speed increased, stride length improved, and her cadence became more regular. Her balance improved, and she was able to perform transfers and stair climbing with less assistance. Her functional independence increased, allowing her to participate more fully in her daily activities.\n\nConclusion\n\nThis case study demonstrates the effectiveness of a comprehensive physiotherapy program in managing Parkinson's disease. By addressing the patient's gait, balance, strength, and functional deficits, physiotherapy can significantly improve mobility, reduce fall risk, and enhance quality of life. Further research is needed to evaluate the long-term benefits of physiotherapy in PD.", "rouge1": 0.264294790343075, "rouge2": 0.05256464603645613, "rougeL": 0.10842863193562051, "bert_precision": 0.7867887020111084, "bert_recall": 0.7604032754898071, "bert_f1": 0.7733710408210754, "gen_words": 564, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.5, "judge_overall": 0.55, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for a PD patient, following a logical flow from presentation through assessment to intervention and outcomes. Clinical findings (shuffling gait, festination, bradykinesia, rigidity) are appropriate and consistent with PD. However, several gaps exist: no standardized outcome measures are cited (e.g., Berg Balance Scale, TUG, UPDRS), no specific dosage/frequency/intensity for interventions is provided, HIIT is mentioned without adequate safety justification for a falls-risk elderly patient, and red flags/contraindications are not addressed. T", "clinical_msk_score": 0.533, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 339, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease - A Musculoskeletal Physiotherapy Perspective\n\nClient: Mr. A.B., Male, 65 years old\n\nPresenting Complaint:\n\nMr. A.B. presented with a 3-month history of progressive gait instability, increasing difficulty with balance, and increasing falls. He reports frequent tripping and near-falls, particularly when walking on uneven surfaces. He also reports increased fatigue and stiffness in his lower limbs, which makes it difficult to walk faster or climb stairs. He also reports that he has difficulty with his balance when standing still and walking, and that he has had difficulty with his balance when standing still and walking. He also reports that he has had difficulty with his balance when standing still and walking, and that he has had difficulty with his balance when standing still and walking.\n\nHistory:\n\nMr. A.B. has a history of Parkinson's Disease for 10 years. He was diagnosed with Parkinson's Disease after a neurological examination and a series of tests. He has been taking medications for Parkinson's Disease for the past 10 years. He also reports having osteoarthritis in his right hip and left knee. He is a retired engineer and has always been active and independent. He lives alone with his wife. He has two adult children who live in another city. He reports having no history of significant trauma. He reports having no history of significant trauma.\n\nExamination Findings:\n\nVital Signs:\n\nBlood Pressure: 130/80 mmHg\n\nHeart Rate: 75 bpm\n\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths/min\n\nOxygen Saturation: 98% on room air\n\nNeurological Examination:\n\nMotor Examination:\n\nPower: There is decreased power in the lower limbs and upper limbs, more pronounced in the lower limbs.\n\nRange of Motion: There is decreased range of motion in the hip and knee joints.\n\nMuscle Tone: There is increased muscle tone in the lower limbs.\n\nGait: There is a shuffling gait with decreased step length and increased step height.\n\nBalance: There is increased postural sway with eyes open and closed.\n\nSensory Examination:\n\nThere is decreased sensation in the lower limbs.\n\nVision: There is decreased visual acuity.\n\nSpeech: There is dysarthria.\n\nCognitive Function: There is mild cognitive impairment.\n\nAssessment:\n\nMr. A.B. has Parkinson's Disease and has decreased power, decreased range of motion, increased muscle tone, decreased step length and increased step height, increased postural sway with eyes open and closed, decreased sensation in the lower limbs, decreased visual acuity, dysarthria, and mild cognitive impairment.\n\nIntervention:\n\n1.  Patient Education: Educated Mr. A.B. about Parkinson's Disease and its effects on gait and balance.\n\n2.  Physical Therapy Interventions:\n\n    *   Gait Training: Gait training was performed using a walker and a balance board. Gait training included exercises for walking on uneven surfaces and walking backwards.\n\n    *   Balance Training: Balance training included exercises for standing on one leg, tandem stance, and walking on a balance board.\n\n    *   Strength Training: Strength training was performed for the lower limbs and upper limbs.\n\n    *   Stretching: Stretching was performed for the lower limbs and upper limbs.\n\n    *   Neuromuscular Re-education: Neuromuscular re-education was performed for the lower limbs and upper limbs.\n\n    *   Balance Training: Balance training was performed using a balance board and balance exercises.\n\n    *   Exercise Program: An exercise program was designed for Mr. A.B. to improve his strength, balance, and gait.\n\nOutcome:\n\nMr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. He reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after 6 weeks of physical therapy. Mr. A.B. reported an improvement in his gait stability and balance after", "rouge1": 0.38756388415672915, "rouge2": 0.0784313725490196, "rougeL": 0.13117546848381603, "bert_precision": 0.7334647178649902, "bert_recall": 0.7327831387519836, "bert_f1": 0.733123779296875, "gen_words": 730, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.2, "judge_overall": 0.18, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "This case study has significant quality issues. The presenting complaint contains obvious copy-paste repetition ('difficulty with his balance when standing still and walking' repeated 4 times). The outcome section is catastrophically repetitive, with the same sentence repeated 11+ times and truncated. The history contains contradictions ('lives alone with his wife'). The gait description is clinically inaccurate for Parkinson's (increased step height is incorrect; festinating gait has decreased step height/clearance). No standardised outcome measures (TUG, BBS, UPDRS) are used. No medication t", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2927, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 340, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease - A Physiotherapy Approach\n\nAbstract\n\nParkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by motor symptoms such as bradykinesia, rigidity, tremor, and postural instability. These symptoms significantly impact quality of life and functional independence, particularly in older adults. Physiotherapy plays a crucial role in managing PD by improving mobility, balance, strength, and overall functional capacity. This case study demonstrates the benefits of a tailored physiotherapy intervention for a patient with PD, highlighting the importance of individualized assessment, exercise-based interventions, and patient education.\n\nCase Presentation\n\nHistory of Presenting Complaint\n\nAn 80-year-old female patient with a history of PD diagnosed 5 years ago, presented to physiotherapy following a fall at home. The fall occurred while getting out of bed in the morning. She reported feeling unsteady and losing her balance. She was transported to the emergency room where she was assessed and found to have no fracture or head injury. She was admitted for observation and discharged home with instructions to see her GP for follow-up.\n\nAt the time of presentation, the patient reported difficulty with gait, increased risk of falls, and reduced mobility. She was using a walker to assist with ambulation. She reported decreased strength in her legs, difficulty initiating movement, and a feeling of stiffness throughout her body. She also reported increased fatigue and decreased sleep. She also reported difficulty with dressing and bathing.\n\nPast Medical History\n\n*   Parkinson's disease diagnosed 5 years ago.\n*   History of hypertension (treated with medication).\n*   History of osteoarthritis in her knees.\n*   History of hypothyroidism (treated with medication).\n*   History of diverticulosis.\n*   History of a previous stroke 10 years ago.\n*   History of diabetes mellitus type 2 (treated with medication).\n*   History of osteoporosis.\n*   History of dementia (mild cognitive impairment).\n\nMedications\n\n*   Levodopa/Carbidopa\n*   Amantadine\n*   Levothyroxine\n*   Amlodipine\n*   Metformin\n*   Alendronate\n*   Furosemide\n\nFamily History\n\n*   No family history of Parkinson's disease.\n\nSocial History\n\n*   Lives alone in her home.\n*   Her daughter assists her with meals and bathing.\n*   She is retired and spends most of her day at home.\n*   She is independent with her activities of daily living (ADLs) except for dressing and bathing.\n*   She enjoys gardening and reading.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\n*   General Appearance: Alert, oriented to person, place, and time, but disoriented to time.\n*   Vitals: BP 145/88 mmHg, HR 82 bpm, RR 16/min, Temp 37.1\u00b0C.\n*   Neurological Examination:\n    *   Motor: Mild bradykinesia, mild rigidity (more pronounced in the lower limbs), mild resting tremor (predominantly in the right hand).\n    *   Sensory: Intact.\n    *   Cranial Nerves: Intact.\n    *   Coordination: Mild difficulty with finger-to-nose testing.\n    *   Gait: Shuffling gait, decreased step length, decreased step height, increased stride time, reduced arm swing.\n    *   Balance: Mild postural instability, decreased ability to maintain balance during tandem stance.\n    *   Strength: Mild weakness in the lower extremities.\n    *   Deep Tendon Reflexes: Normal.\n    *   Babinski sign: Negative.\n*   Musculoskeletal Examination:\n    *   Range of Motion: Decreased range of motion in the hips, knees, and ankles.\n    *   Muscle Strength: Mild weakness in the lower extremities.\n    *   Sensation: Intact.\n    *   Palpation: No areas of tenderness.\n    *   Gait Analysis: Shuffling gait, decreased step length, decreased step height, increased stride time, reduced arm swing.\n\nImaging\n\n*   X-ray of the lumbar spine: No evidence of fracture or spinal stenosis.\n\nIntervention\n\nIntervention included:\n\n1.  Exercise Therapy:\n    *   Balance exercises: Standing on one leg, tandem stance, walking heel-to-toe.\n    *   Strength training: Leg extension, leg flexion, hip abduction, hip adduction, sit-to-stand transfers.\n    *   Gait training: Walking with walker, walking heel-to-toe, walking with visual cues.\n    *   Flexibility exercises: Hamstring stretch, quadriceps stretch, hip flexor stretch.\n    *   Aerobic exercise: Brisk walking in the hallway.\n    *   Functional exercises: Getting out of bed, getting up from a chair, getting up from a toilet.\n\n", "rouge1": 0.03858520900321544, "rouge2": 0.00967741935483871, "rougeL": 0.035369774919614155, "bert_precision": 0.640103280544281, "bert_recall": 0.7273097038269043, "bert_f1": 0.6809256672859192, "gen_words": 624, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.5, "judge_overall": 0.52, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a clinically coherent narrative for an 80-year-old with PD following a fall. Strengths include detailed PMH, medications, and physical examination findings consistent with PD. However, several significant gaps exist: (1) No formal outcome measures used (e.g., Berg Balance Scale, TUG, UPDRS, MDS-UPDRS); (2) Treatment plan lacks dosage, frequency, duration, intensity, and progression details; (3) No mention of LSVT BIG or other PD-specific evidence-based protocols; (4) Cognitive impairment and osteoporosis are not adequately addressed in the intervention; (5) No goal-sett", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4967, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 341, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Title: Physiotherapy Management of Gait and Balance Impairment in a Patient with Parkinson's Disease\n\nIntroduction\n\nParkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra, leading to motor symptoms such as bradykinesia, rigidity, tremor, and postural instability. Gait and balance impairment are common and significant comorbidities in PD, impacting quality of life, increasing fall risk, and contributing to reduced mobility and independence. This case study illustrates the successful application of a comprehensive physiotherapy program to improve gait and balance in a patient with PD.\n\nCase Presentation\n\nPatient Profile\n\nA 68-year-old male with a 5-year history of Parkinson's disease presented for physiotherapy evaluation. His diagnosis was confirmed by a neurologist, and he was currently managed with levodopa and carbidopa. He was independent in most daily activities but reported difficulty walking short distances, especially on uneven surfaces. His family members also reported increased falls in the past 6 months, with one fall resulting in a fractured wrist.\n\nGait and Balance Assessment\n\nInitial assessment revealed the following:\n\nGait characteristics:\n\n*   Shuffling gait with reduced stride length and increased cadence.\n*   Reduced hip and knee flexion during stance phase.\n*   Reduced forward trunk lean during swing phase.\n*   Reduced pelvic drop during stance phase.\n*   Reduced hip adduction during stance phase.\n*   Reduced knee flexion during stance phase.\n*   Reduced ankle dorsiflexion during stance phase.\n*   Increased forward trunk lean during swing phase.\n*   Increased pelvic drop during stance phase.\n*   Increased hip adduction during stance phase.\n*   Increased knee flexion during stance phase.\n*   Increased ankle dorsiflexion during stance phase.\n*   Reduced step length.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Reduced stride length.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   Reduced stride width.\n*   Increased cadence.\n*   Reduced stride time.\n*   ", "rouge1": 0.21978021978021975, "rouge2": 0.026026026026026026, "rougeL": 0.07192807192807193, "bert_precision": 0.7416924238204956, "bert_recall": 0.7342885732650757, "bert_f1": 0.7379719614982605, "gen_words": 623, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.0, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.05, "judge_overall": 0.08, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study has a reasonable introduction and patient profile with appropriate clinical context for Parkinson's disease. However, the gait assessment section devolves into massive, nonsensical repetition - the same bullet points ('Increased cadence', 'Reduced stride time', 'Reduced stride width') are repeated dozens of times, and there are contradictory findings listed (both 'reduced' and 'increased' for the same parameters like trunk lean, pelvic drop, hip adduction, knee flexion, ankle dorsiflexion). The case study appears to be truncated/corrupted with no treatment plan, no outcome measu", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2003, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 342, "title": "Parkinson's", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Parkinson's Disease - A Physiotherapy Approach\n\nThis case study highlights the potential of physiotherapy in managing the multifaceted symptoms of Parkinson's disease.\n\nAbstract\n\nParkinson's disease (PD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder primarily affecting motor function. This case study details the evaluation, treatment, and outcome of a 73-year-old male patient presenting with symptoms of PD. The patient was assessed for functional limitations, gait disturbances, balance problems, and cognitive deficits. A combination of exercise therapy, education, and environmental modifications were implemented. The patient demonstrated significant improvements in gait speed, balance, and functional independence, highlighting the importance of a comprehensive, multidisciplinary approach to PD management.\n\nPatient Presentation\n\nA 73-year-old male, Mr. R, presented to the outpatient physiotherapy clinic with a 3-month history of symptoms suggestive of Parkinson's disease. His wife reported a gradual onset of difficulty initiating movements, shuffling gait, and episodes of freezing of gait. Mr. R reported feeling increasingly fatigued and having difficulty with activities of daily living (ADLs) such as dressing and bathing. He also reported experiencing mild cognitive impairment and depression.\n\nExamination Findings\n\nUpon examination, Mr. R exhibited the classic signs of Parkinson's disease: bradykinesia (slowness of movement), rigidity (increased muscle tone), and postural instability (impaired balance). His gait was characterized by a shuffling pattern, reduced stride length, and freezing of gait, particularly when transitioning from standing to walking. He demonstrated impaired balance, with increased sway during tandem stance and difficulty rising from a chair. His upper limb movements were also reduced, with decreased amplitude and speed. Strength was preserved throughout, but his range of motion was restricted. His cognitive assessment revealed mild deficits in executive function and memory.\n\nIntervention\n\nThe primary goal of physiotherapy was to improve functional mobility, balance, and reduce the risk of falls. The intervention program included:\n\n1.  **Exercise Therapy:**\n    *   **Aerobic Exercise:** Gentle aerobic exercises, such as walking and stationary cycling, were introduced to improve cardiovascular fitness and endurance.\n    *   **Balance Training:** Exercises targeting balance, such as standing on one leg, tandem stance, and weight shifts, were performed on a stable surface.\n    *   **Strength Training:** Isometric and resistance exercises were used to improve muscle strength and endurance, particularly in the lower limbs.\n    *   **Gait Training:** Exercises focused on improving gait speed, stride length, and reducing freezing of gait. This included cueing strategies (e.g., using auditory cues) to overcome freezing episodes.\n    *   **Flexibility and Stretching:** Gentle stretching exercises were performed to maintain range of motion and reduce stiffness.\n\n2.  **Education:**\n    *   Mr. R and his wife were educated about Parkinson's disease, its progression, and the importance of physiotherapy.\n    *   They were taught strategies to manage freezing of gait, such as using visual cues or stepping over small obstacles.\n    *   They were educated on the importance of maintaining a regular exercise routine and healthy diet.\n\n3.  **Environmental Modifications:**\n    *   The home environment was assessed for potential hazards, such as poor lighting and cluttered pathways.\n    *   Adaptive equipment, such as a raised toilet seat and grab bars, was recommended.\n\nOutcome\n\nAfter an 8-week physiotherapy program, Mr. R demonstrated significant improvements:\n\n*   **Gait:** His gait speed increased by 20%, and his shuffling pattern was reduced. Freezing of gait episodes decreased significantly.\n*   **Balance:** His balance improved, as evidenced by reduced sway during tandem stance.\n*   **Functional Independence:** He was able to perform ADLs with greater ease and independence.\n*   **Mood:** His wife reported an improvement in his mood and overall well-being.\n\nConclusion\n\nThis case study demonstrates the effectiveness of a comprehensive physiotherapy approach in managing Parkinson's disease. Exercise therapy, education, and environmental modifications can significantly improve functional mobility, balance, and quality of life for individuals with PD. It is crucial to tailor the intervention program to the individual's needs and capabilities, and to provide ongoing support and education.", "rouge1": 0.3815580286168521, "rouge2": 0.0445859872611465, "rougeL": 0.15421303656597776, "bert_precision": 0.7649571895599365, "bert_recall": 0.7563626766204834, "bert_f1": 0.7606356739997864, "gen_words": 622, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.5, "judge_overall": 0.55, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for Parkinson's disease with logical flow from presentation to intervention. Clinical findings are appropriate (bradykinesia, rigidity, postural instability, freezing of gait). However, several gaps exist: no standardized outcome measures are cited (e.g., Berg Balance Scale, UPDRS, TUG test), no medication history or neurologist involvement is mentioned, differential diagnosis is absent, and treatment dosage/frequency/intensity/progression details are largely missing. Balance training is only on stable surfaces - no progression to unstable ", "clinical_msk_score": 0.535, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
