{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 376, "title": "Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV) is a common cause of vertigo, and it is caused by dislodged otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals) in the semicircular canals of the inner ear. These crystals move when the head is repositioned, causing brief episodes of dizziness.\n\nThis case study describes the history, examination findings, diagnosis, and treatment of a patient with BPPV.\n\nPatient: A 58-year-old male with a history of osteoarthritis, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia.\n\nHistory:\nThe patient presented to the physiotherapy clinic with a 2-week history of sudden onset vertigo that occurs when changing position, particularly when lying down or rolling over in bed. The vertigo is brief, lasting less than a minute, and accompanied by a sense of spinning. He also experiences nausea and vomiting. The patient reports that the vertigo started after he lifted a heavy box and fell. He has no history of head trauma or ear infections. He is taking atorvastatin, lisinopril, and aspirin.\n\nExamination:\nThe patient's neurological examination is normal. Visual acuity and cranial nerve examination are intact. There are no focal neurological deficits. The patient's gait is normal.\n\nDuring the examination, the patient's head is moved in various positions. When the patient lies down, his head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the