 patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness. During this maneuver, the patient's head is turned to the right, and his right ear is elevated. The patient reports dizziness", "rouge1": 0.28470380194518125, "rouge2": 0.06487761722205838, "rougeL": 0.15266725611553195, "bert_precision": 0.7878440618515015, "bert_recall": 0.780893087387085, "bert_f1": 0.7843531966209412, "gen_words": 780, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.1, "judge_overall": 0.1, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study has a reasonable introduction and patient history for BPPV, but suffers from severe quality issues. The examination section contains massive repetitive text - the same sentence about head positioning is repeated approximately 30+ times, indicating a generation error. There is no proper Dix-Hallpike test description with nystagmus findings, no mention of which canal is affected, no differential diagnosis, no diagnostic impression section, and critically no treatment plan whatsoever (no canalith repositioning maneuver like Epley, no vestibular rehabilitation, no dosage/frequency/p", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2421, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 377, "title": "Wilson's Disease", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Wilson's Disease is a rare genetic disorder that causes copper to accumulate in the liver, brain, eyes, and other vital organs. It can lead to a variety of health problems, including liver disease, neurological disorders, and eye problems.\n\nWilson's Disease is a complex condition, and physiotherapy can play an important role in managing its symptoms and improving quality of life. Physiotherapy can help to improve mobility, reduce pain, improve balance, and restore function.\n\nA physiotherapist can assess a person's mobility, strength, and balance, and provide exercises and interventions to improve these areas. Physiotherapy can also help to manage the fatigue and pain that can be associated with Wilson's Disease.\n\nIt is important to work with a physiotherapist who is knowledgeable about Wilson's Disease. They can provide tailored exercises and interventions to meet the individual's needs.\n\nThe main symptoms of Wilson's Disease are:\n\n*   **Liver disease:** This can range from mild liver enlargement to severe liver failure.\n*   **Neurological symptoms:** These can include tremor, difficulty walking, coordination problems, and cognitive issues.\n*   **Eye problems:** These can include Kayser-Fleischer rings, which are copper deposits in the cornea.\n*   **Kidney problems:** This can include kidney failure.\n*   **Other symptoms:** These can include fatigue, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and weight loss.\n\nThere is no cure for Wilson's Disease, but there are treatments that can help to manage the condition and prevent complications. Treatment usually involves a combination of medication and dietary changes.\n\nWilson's Disease is a progressive condition, meaning that it can worsen over time. It is important to seek medical attention early to prevent complications.\n\nPhysiotherapy can play an important role in managing the symptoms of Wilson's Disease. It can help to improve mobility, reduce pain, and restore function.\n\nIt is important to work with a physiotherapist who is knowledgeable about Wilson's Disease. They can provide tailored exercises and interventions to meet the individual's needs.\n\nThere are many different types of physiotherapy treatments that can be used to manage Wilson's Disease. Some of the most common treatments include:\n\n*   **Exercise therapy:** This can help to improve strength, flexibility, and balance.\n*   **Manual therapy:** This can help to relieve pain and improve joint mobility.\n*   **Electrotherapy:** This can help to reduce muscle spasms and inflammation.\n*   **Hydrotherapy:** This can help to improve strength and flexibility in a warm, supportive environment.\n\nThe main goal of physiotherapy is to help patients with Wilson's Disease to live as independently as possible.\n\nA physiotherapist can help patients to develop strategies to manage their condition and improve their quality of life.\n\nWilson's Disease can cause a variety of symptoms, including liver disease, neurological symptoms, and eye problems. Physiotherapy can help to manage these symptoms and improve quality of life.\n\nIt is important to work with a physiotherapist who is knowledgeable about Wilson's Disease. They can provide tailored exercises and interventions to meet the individual's needs.\n\nThere are many different types of physiotherapy treatments that can be used to manage Wilson's Disease. Some of the most common treatments include:\n\n*   Exercise therapy\n*   Manual therapy\n*   Electrotherapy\n*   Hydrotherapy\n\nThe main goal of physiotherapy is to help patients with Wilson's Disease to live as independently as possible.\n\nA physiotherapist can help patients to develop strategies to manage their condition and improve their quality of life.\n\nThe main symptoms of Wilson's Disease are:\n\n*   Liver disease\n*   Neurological symptoms\n*   Eye problems\n*   Kidney problems\n*   Other symptoms\n\nThere is no cure for Wilson's Disease, but there are treatments that can help to manage the condition and prevent complications. Treatment usually involves a combination of medication and dietary changes.\n\nWilson's Disease is a progressive condition, meaning that it can worsen over time. It is important to seek medical attention early to prevent complications.\n\nPhysiotherapy can play an important role in managing the symptoms of Wilson's Disease. It can help to improve mobility, reduce pain, and restore function.\n\nIt is important to work with a physiotherapist who is knowledgeable about Wilson's Disease. They can provide tailored exercises and interventions to meet the individual's needs.\n\nThere are many different types of physiotherapy treatments that can be used to manage Wilson's Disease. Some of the most common treatments include:\n\n*   Exercise therapy\n*   Manual therapy\n*   Electrotherapy\n*   Hydrotherapy\n\nThe main goal of physiotherapy is to help patients with Wilson's Disease to live as independently as possible.\n\nA physiotherapist can help patients to develop strategies to manage their condition and improve their quality of life.\n\nThe main symptoms of Wilson's Disease are:\n\n*   Liver disease\n*   Neurological symptoms\n*   Eye problems\n", "rouge1": 0.24635264693622344, "rouge2": 0.0725604670558799, "rougeL": 0.106294289287203, "bert_precision": 0.7842615842819214, "bert_recall": 0.7401114702224731, "bert_f1": 0.7615471482276917, "gen_words": 757, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.05, "judge_overall": 0.08, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "This case study is extremely poor quality. It lacks any actual patient presentation, subjective/objective assessment, clinical reasoning, or specific treatment plan. It is highly repetitive, with entire sections copy-pasted multiple times. There is no patient history, no physical examination findings, no outcome measures, no differential diagnosis, no special tests, and no treatment dosage or progression. Wilson's Disease is primarily a metabolic/neurological condition, not an MSK condition, and the case study fails to address physiotherapy-specific management in any meaningful way. The treatm", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2084, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 378, "title": "Wrist Pain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Wrist Pain in a 55-Year-Old Female\n\nAbstract\n\nThis case study presents the rehabilitation of a 55-year-old female patient who presented with right wrist pain. The patient had been experiencing pain and stiffness for three months following a fall from a ladder. Her symptoms had significantly impacted her daily activities and sleep. Examination revealed tenderness at the distal radius, limited range of motion, and weakness in grip strength. Initial treatment involved modalities and soft tissue mobilization, followed by progressive strengthening and stretching exercises. The patient achieved significant pain reduction and functional improvement within six weeks of treatment.\n\nIntroduction\n\nWrist pain is a common musculoskeletal complaint that can result from various causes, including trauma, overuse, and underlying pathology. In this case, the patient experienced wrist pain following a fall. The purpose of this case study is to detail the assessment, treatment, and outcome of a patient with wrist pain.\n\nCase Presentation\n\nHistory\n\nA 55-year-old female with a history of rheumatoid arthritis (RA) presented to the clinic complaining of right wrist pain. The patient reported that she had been experiencing pain and stiffness for three months following a fall from a ladder. The fall occurred approximately three months prior, and she had subsequently experienced a sharp pain in her right wrist. She had been diagnosed with RA approximately three years prior and had been taking medication for this condition. She also reported that she had been experiencing increased pain in her left wrist and shoulder.\n\nExamination\n\nOn examination, the patient's right wrist was swollen and tender to palpation at the distal radius. There was limited range of motion in both the wrist and elbow. Her left wrist and shoulder were also swollen and tender. There was decreased grip strength in both hands, but the decrease was greater on the right side. There was also tenderness and swelling in the left elbow.\n\nImaging\n\nX-rays of the wrist were obtained, and they showed a fracture of the distal radius.\n\nTreatment\n\nThe patient was treated with modalities, soft tissue mobilization, and progressive strengthening exercises. She was also given an exercise program to improve her range of motion and strength.\n\nOutcome\n\nThe patient experienced significant pain reduction and functional improvement within six weeks of treatment. Her grip strength improved, and her range of motion returned to normal. She was able to return to her normal activities of daily living.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe patient experienced wrist pain following a fall. The examination revealed tenderness at the distal radius and limited range of motion. X-rays confirmed a distal radius fracture. The patient received treatment that included modalities, soft tissue mobilization, and progressive strengthening exercises. She experienced significant pain reduction and functional improvement within six weeks of treatment.\n\nConclusion\n\nThe patient experienced wrist pain following a fall. The examination revealed tenderness at the distal radius and limited range of motion. X-rays confirmed a distal radius fracture. The patient received treatment that included modalities, soft tissue mobilization, and progressive strengthening exercises. She experienced significant pain reduction and functional improvement within six weeks of treatment.\n\nKeywords: wrist pain, distal radius fracture, rehabilitation, physiotherapy, rheumatoid arthritis, exercise therapy\n\nReferences\n\n1.  [1]Clinical Guidelines for the Management of Distal Radius Fractures: A Report of the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, Hand and Upper Extremity Care Committee. Journal of the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, 2003;11(1):1-32.\n\n2.  [2]M. C. L. R. R. H. C. S. R. A. H. L. S. R. A. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R. R.", "rouge1": 0.276173285198556, "rouge2": 0.05063291139240507, "rougeL": 0.1263537906137184, "bert_precision": 0.7928321361541748, "bert_recall": 0.7737202644348145, "bert_f1": 0.7831595540046692, "gen_words": 714, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.2, "judge_overall": 0.22, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "A confirmed distal radius fracture is treated with soft tissue mobilization and progressive strengthening without any mention of fracture healing status, immobilization, orthopedic clearance, or weight-bearing precautions. Applying mobilization and strengthening to an unhealed or inadequately manage", "judge_reasoning": "This case study has several significant clinical issues. A distal radius fracture in a patient with RA presenting 3 months post-injury is a major red flag - fracture management should involve immobilization/orthopedic referral before physiotherapy, yet this is not addressed. The treatment description is extremely vague with no dosage, frequency, intensity, or specific interventions specified. The RA comorbidity is mentioned but not integrated into clinical reasoning or treatment modifications. Special tests are absent. Differential diagnosis is not explored. The examination findings (bilateral", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3163, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 381, "title": "Calf Muscle Tear", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Calf Muscle Tear\n\nAbstract\n\nThis case study details the rehabilitation of a 45-year-old male professional soccer player who sustained a mid-shaft tear of the left gastrocnemius muscle. The patient presented with acute pain, swelling, and inability to perform his normal soccer activities. The initial management included RICE (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation), followed by gradual progression of exercises and interventions over a period of 8 weeks. The patient experienced significant pain reduction and improved strength and range of motion over the rehabilitation period. At the end of the 8-week rehabilitation period, the patient was cleared to return to soccer with no restrictions.\n\nPatient Information\n\nPatient: 45-year-old male professional soccer player\n\nOccupation: Professional Soccer Player\n\nPresenting Complaint\n\nThe patient reported a sudden, sharp pain in the left calf during a soccer practice session. He felt a \"pop\" sensation in the muscle, followed by immediate swelling and inability to walk. He was unable to perform any soccer activities and required assistance to ambulate.\n\nPast Medical History\n\nThe patient had no significant past medical history. He was a recreational runner before injury, but recently started training with the professional team. He was treated with an NSAID for the initial pain and swelling.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nVital Signs\n\nTemperature: 37.1\u00b0C (98.8\u00b0F)\n\nHeart Rate: 84 bpm\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nRight Calf: Normal range of motion and strength.\n\nLeft Calf:\n\n- Pain: Moderate pain along the mid-shaft of the gastrocnemius muscle.\n\n- Swelling: Marked swelling and tenderness along the mid-shaft of the gastrocnemius.\n\n- Range of Motion: Limited active and passive range of motion due to pain.\n\n- Strength: Weakness in plantarflexion.\n\n- Palpation: Palpable defect along the mid-shaft of the gastrocnemius.\n\nImaging\n\nAn MRI of the left calf was performed. The MRI confirmed a mid-shaft tear of the gastrocnemius muscle.\n\nTreatment Plan\n\nInitial Management\n\n1.  Ice: Applied for 15-20 minutes multiple times per day.\n2.  Compression: Elastic wrap applied to the calf.\n3.  Elevation: Calf elevated above the heart level.\n4.  Pain Medication: NSAIDs prescribed.\n5.  Rest: Patient was instructed to rest the injured leg and avoid any activities that aggravated the pain.\n\nRehabilitation Phase\n\nThe patient began a progressive rehabilitation program over 8 weeks.\n\nPhase 1: (Weeks 1-2)\n\n-   Passive Range of Motion: Gradual increase in passive range of motion exercises.\n-   Active-Assisted Range of Motion: Patient assisted with exercises using a stick or parallel bars.\n-   Isometric Exercises: Exercises performed without joint movement.\n-   Pain Management: Continued use of ice and NSAIDs.\n\nPhase 2: (Weeks 3-4)\n\n-   Active Range of Motion: Patient performed range of motion exercises independently.\n-   Strengthening Exercises: Progressive strengthening exercises for the calf muscles.\n-   Stretching Exercises: Stretching exercises for the calf muscles.\n-   Functional Exercises: Exercises that mimic soccer movements.\n\nPhase 3: (Weeks 5-6)\n\n-   Progressive Strengthening Exercises: Increased resistance and repetitions.\n-   Return to Soccer-Specific Drills: Gradual return to soccer-specific drills.\n-   Increased Range of Motion: Further increase in range of motion exercises.\n-   Stretching Exercises: Increased intensity and duration of stretching exercises.\n\nPhase 4: (Weeks 7-8)\n\n-   Return to Full Soccer Training: Gradual return to full soccer training.\n-   Increased Intensity and Duration: Increased intensity and duration of soccer-specific drills.\n-   Continued Stretching and Strengthening: Continued stretching and strengthening exercises.\n\nOutcome\n\nAt the end of the 8-week rehabilitation period, the patient reported a significant decrease in pain and swelling. He was able to perform range of motion exercises without pain. His strength and range of motion were within normal limits. He was able to perform soccer-specific drills without pain and was cleared to return to soccer with no restrictions.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThis case study illustrates the successful rehabilitation of a mid-shaft calf muscle tear in a professional soccer player. The patient experienced significant pain and swelling, which were managed with RICE and pain medication. The progressive rehabilitation program allowed the patient to gradually return to his previous level of function and activity.\n\nConclusion\n\nThis case study demonstrates that a structured rehabilitation program is effective in the treatment of mid-shaft calf muscle tears. The rehabilitation program should be tailored to the individual patient's needs and should progress gradually to allow for optimal recovery and return to activity.", "rouge1": 0.2465462274176408, "rouge2": 0.057507987220447275, "rougeL": 0.1232731137088204, "bert_precision": 0.7799850702285767, "bert_recall": 0.7814444303512573, "bert_f1": 0.7807140946388245, "gen_words": 692, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.42, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a logical flow from injury to rehabilitation but has several notable gaps. Clinical coherence is reasonable but lacks subjective outcome measures (e.g., VISA-G, FAAM) and grading of tear severity. Diagnostic accuracy is weak: no differential diagnosis is mentioned (e.g., DVT, Achilles tendon rupture, soleus tear), no special tests documented (Thompson test, squeeze test), and MRI findings lack specificity (grade of tear). Treatment completeness is poor: no specific dosage parameters (sets, reps, frequency), no criteria-based progression, no neuromuscular or propriocepti", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4555, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 392, "title": "Ankle Sprain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Title: Acute Ankle Sprain in an Active Young Adult\n\nAbstract\n\nThis case study presents a 22-year-old male who sustained an acute ankle sprain during a basketball game. He was treated with a combination of manual therapy, exercise therapy, and education, achieving significant pain relief and functional recovery within 8 weeks. This case demonstrates the effectiveness of a structured rehabilitation approach in managing acute ankle sprains.\n\nClinical Presentation\n\nHistory\n\nA 22-year-old male, a college student and part-time basketball player, presents with a 3-day history of right ankle pain following a basketball game. He landed awkwardly on his right foot while attempting a cut, resulting in a twisting motion and immediate pain, swelling, and tenderness. He was unable to bear weight initially but was able to walk with a limp after 24 hours. He describes the pain as sharp and localized to the lateral aspect of his ankle. He has no history of previous ankle injuries, but has had minor ankle sprains in the past. He denies any numbness, tingling, or weakness. He has no significant past medical history, takes no medications, and has no allergies. He is currently employed as a full-time student.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nVitals were stable. The right ankle was swollen and erythematous. Tenderness was present over the lateral malleolus, anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) and calcaneofibular ligament (CFL) with a positive Thompson test. The range of motion was limited due to pain, particularly dorsiflexion and plantarflexion. Muscle strength was preserved in all planes. Neurological examination was intact.\n\nDiagnosis\n\nBased on the history and physical examination findings, the patient was diagnosed with a right acute lateral ankle sprain, primarily involving the ATFL and CFL, with some involvement of the posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL).\n\nTreatment Plan\n\nTreatment was initiated immediately following diagnosis.\n\n1.  Ice and compression were applied to the ankle for 15 minutes, 3 times per day.\n2.  Manual therapy was performed to address soft tissue swelling and muscle guarding.\n3.  Exercise therapy was introduced to gradually restore range of motion, strength, and proprioception.\n4.  Patient education regarding ankle sprains, prevention, and return to activity was provided.\n\nOutcome\n\nWithin 8 weeks, the patient experienced significant improvement.\n\nPain decreased from a score of 8/10 to 1/10.\nSwelling decreased to 2/10.\nRange of motion improved from 15 degrees of dorsiflexion to 70 degrees.\nStrength improved from 3/10 to 8/10.\nProprioception improved from 4/10 to 8/10.\nReturn to basketball was achieved at 6 weeks.\n\nConclusion\n\nThis case demonstrates the effectiveness of a structured rehabilitation approach in managing acute ankle sprains. A combination of manual therapy, exercise therapy, and education is effective in achieving pain relief and functional recovery. The patient was able to return to basketball at 6 weeks, indicating that the ankle was sufficiently healed for safe participation.", "rouge1": 0.369109947643979, "rouge2": 0.11548556430446195, "rougeL": 0.16753926701570682, "bert_precision": 0.7881578803062439, "bert_recall": 0.7889180779457092, "bert_f1": 0.7885378003120422, "gen_words": 456, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.42, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a reasonable clinical narrative for an acute lateral ankle sprain. Clinical coherence is adequate with logical flow from history to diagnosis to treatment. However, several issues reduce quality: (1) Thompson test is inappropriate here - it tests Achilles tendon rupture, not ankle ligament integrity; correct tests would be anterior drawer and talar tilt. (2) The diagnosis mentions PTFL involvement without clinical justification. (3) Treatment plan lacks specificity - no dosage, frequency, progression details for manual therapy or exercises. (4) Outcome measures use non-", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4615, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 398, "title": "Calf Muscle Tear", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Calf Muscle Tear\n\nPatient: Mr. A.J.S.\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n\nMr. A.J.S. is a 54-year-old male, a retired construction worker, who presented to the physiotherapy clinic with acute onset right calf pain following a sports injury. He was playing recreational football with his friends at a local park when he attempted to stop suddenly to avoid a collision with another player. During this maneuver, he felt a sharp pain in his right calf, followed by immediate cessation of activity. He was able to bear weight on his right leg but experienced significant pain, particularly with any movement involving the calf muscle. He also reported swelling in his right calf and lower leg, and some bruising. He had no previous history of calf muscle tears, although he did have a history of mild calf strains during his construction career. He has no history of significant medical conditions, no allergies, and no medications.\n\nExamination Findings:\n\nSubjective:\n\nPain Scale: 0/10 at rest, 6/10 with walking and 8/10 with active plantarflexion.\n\nRange of Motion:\n\nThere is marked pain and limitation with active plantarflexion, dorsiflexion, and knee flexion. Passive plantarflexion and dorsiflexion are possible but painful.\n\nMuscle Strength:\n\nThere is significant weakness in the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles, particularly with active plantarflexion (strength 2/10) and knee flexion (strength 3/10). There is also some weakness in the tibialis anterior and posterior muscles with active dorsiflexion (strength 3/10).\n\nGait:\n\nPatient is able to ambulate with the assistance of a walking aid (crutches) due to pain and weakness. He is favouring his right leg and walking with a slight limp.\n\nNeurological:\n\nThere is no focal neurological deficit on examination.\n\nPalpation:\n\nThere is significant tenderness along the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles. There is also swelling and bruising noted over the right calf.\n\nSpecial Tests:\n\n*   Passive plantarflexion test: Pain and limitation with passive plantarflexion.\n*   Passive dorsiflexion test: Pain and limitation with passive dorsiflexion.\n*   Knee flexion test: Pain and limitation with knee flexion.\n*   Nilsson's test: Positive for gastrocnemius tightness.\n*   Bahr's test: Positive for soleus tightness.\n*   Weber's test: Negative for right leg.\n*   Squeezing the calf muscle: Pain and weakness.\n\nImaging:\n\nAn ultrasound scan was performed, which confirmed a tear of the gastrocnemius muscle. The tear was identified as a partial tear with a small hematoma.\n\nTreatment Plan:\n\n1.  Acute Phase (First 2-3 weeks):\n\n    *   Pain and inflammation management: Ice, compression, elevation, and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) were prescribed.\n    *   Protection: Crutches were provided to reduce weight-bearing and allow the calf muscle to rest.\n    *   Modalities: Ultrasound and heat were used to promote healing.\n    *   Range of Motion: Gentle passive and active-assisted range of motion exercises were performed to maintain joint mobility and prevent contractures.\n    *   Strengthening: Gentle isometric exercises were performed to maintain muscle tone without causing further irritation.\n    *   Stretching: Gentle stretching of the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles was performed.\n    *   Education: Education on the importance of rest, proper skin care, and gradual return to activity was provided.\n\n2.  Subacute Phase (Weeks 3-6):\n\n    *   Progression of exercises: Range of motion, strengthening, and stretching exercises were gradually progressed.\n    *   Return to activity: The patient was encouraged to gradually return to his normal activities.\n    *   Functional training: Exercises were performed to improve balance, proprioception, and gait mechanics.\n    *   Scar tissue management: Scar tissue massage was performed to improve tissue mobility.\n\n3.  Return to Activity Phase (Weeks 6-8):\n\n    *   Gradual return to sport: The patient was allowed to gradually return to his normal activities, including playing recreational football.\n    *   Sport-specific training: Exercises were performed to improve performance and prevent re-injury.\n    *   Continued scar tissue management: Scar tissue massage was continued.\n    *Outcome: Mr. A.J.S. had a full recovery from his calf muscle tear and was able to return to playing football within 8 weeks.\n\nConclusion:\n\nMr. A.J.S. had a satisfactory recovery from a calf muscle tear. The injury was diagnosed following a thorough history, physical examination, and ultrasound imaging. The patient was managed with a structured physiotherapy", "rouge1": 0.22407628128724671, "rouge2": 0.06212664277180406, "rougeL": 0.11918951132300358, "bert_precision": 0.7663652300834656, "bert_recall": 0.7367843389511108, "bert_f1": 0.7512837052345276, "gen_words": 661, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.35, "judge_overall": 0.42, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Heat modality is recommended in the acute phase (first 2-3 weeks), which is contraindicated as it can increase inflammation and hematoma size in acute muscle tears. Aggressive stretching in the acute phase of a partial tear may also cause further tissue damage.", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a reasonable clinical narrative for a calf muscle tear with logical flow from history to examination to treatment. However, several issues exist: (1) Muscle strength graded '2/10' and '3/10' is non-standard (MRC scale is 0-5); (2) Special tests listed (Nilsson's, Bahr's, Weber's) are not well-established evidence-based tests for calf tears - Thompson/Simmonds test for Achilles rupture is notably absent; (3) Heat in the acute phase is contraindicated; (4) Stretching in the acute phase (first 2-3 weeks) of a partial tear is questionable; (5) Treatment lacks specific dosag", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4317, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 403, "title": "Ankle Sprain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Ankle Sprain\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Age:** 22\n*   **Sex:** Male\n*   **Occupation:** Student\n*   **Presenting Complaint:** Right ankle pain after twisting injury while playing basketball.\n\n**History:**\n\n*   **Onset:** Occurred during a basketball game when he landed awkwardly after a jump.\n*   **Mechanism:** Twisting injury of the right ankle.\n*   **Symptoms:** Immediate pain, swelling, bruising, difficulty walking, inability to bear weight.\n*   **Pain Scale:** 8/10 at rest, 10/10 with weight bearing.\n*   **Duration:** 3 days.\n*   **Pre-Injury:** No prior ankle injuries or surgeries.\n*   **Past Medical History:** No significant medical history.\n*   **Medications:** None.\n*   **Allergies:** None.\n*   **Social History:** Non-smoker, occasional alcohol consumption.\n\n**Examination Findings:**\n\n*   **Inspection:** Swelling and ecchymosis (bruising) present on the lateral aspect of the right ankle.\n*   **Palpation:** Tenderness to palpation over the lateral ligaments of the ankle (anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL), calcaneofibular ligament (CFL), posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)).\n*   **Range of Motion:**\n    *   **Active:** Limited dorsiflexion and plantarflexion due to pain.\n    *   **Passive:** Limited dorsiflexion and plantarflexion due to pain and swelling.\n    *   **Strength:** Mild weakness in dorsiflexion and plantarflexion due to pain and guarding.\n    *   **Special Tests:**\n        *   **Tinel's Test:** Positive (pain reproduction over the ATFL).\n        *   **Homan's Sign:** Negative.\n        *   **Weber's Test:** Positive (pain and tenderness on the lateral side of the ankle).\n        *   **Anterior Drawer Test:** Positive (anterior translation of the talus).\n        *   **Posterior Drawer Test:** Negative.\n        *   **External Rotation Test:** Positive (pain reproduction).\n        *   **External Rotation Stress Test:** Positive (pain reproduction).\n        *   **Varus Stress Test:** Negative.\n        *   **Valgus Stress Test:** Negative.\n*   **Neurovascular Assessment:** Intact peripheral pulses, sensation, and motor function.\n*   **Imaging:** X-rays of the right ankle were taken and showed no evidence of fracture or dislocation.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n*   Right Ankle Sprain, Grade II (Ligament Sprain)\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\n*   **Acute Phase (First 72 hours):**\n    *   **RICE Protocol:**\n        *   **Rest:** Non-weight bearing using crutches.\n        *   **Ice:** Apply ice packs to the affected area for 15-20 minutes every 2-3 hours.\n        *   **Compression:** Apply an elastic bandage to reduce swelling.\n        *   **Elevation:** Elevate the ankle above the level of the heart.\n    *   **Medication:** NSAIDs (e.g., ibuprofen) for pain and inflammation.\n    *   **Physiotherapy:**\n        *   **Manual Therapy:** Gentle soft tissue mobilization and joint mobilization of the ankle.\n        *   **Modalities:** Ultrasound and TENS for pain relief and muscle relaxation.\n        *   **Passive Range of Motion:** Gentle passive range of motion exercises to maintain joint mobility.\n        *   **Active-Assisted Range of Motion:** Encouraging the patient to actively move the ankle, with assistance from the therapist if needed.\n        *   **Strengthening:** Light isometric exercises for ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n        *   **Neuromuscular Re-education:** Exercises to restore normal gait pattern and muscle control.\n*   **Subacute Phase (After 72 hours):**\n    *   **Continue RICE Protocol:** As needed for swelling.\n    *   **Continue Physiotherapy:**\n        *   **Strengthening:** Progressive strengthening exercises for ankle dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, inversion, and eversion.\n        *   **Proprioception/Balance:** Exercises to improve balance and ankle stability (e.g., single leg stance, wobble board).\n        *   **Aerobic Conditioning:** Low-impact aerobic exercises (e.g., stationary cycling, swimming) to improve cardiovascular fitness and reduce swelling.\n        *   **Return to Activity:** Gradual progression of activities, starting with low-impact activities and gradually increasing intensity and duration.\n*   **Chronic Phase (After 4-6 weeks):**\n    *   **Continue Physi", "rouge1": 0.2903225806451613, "rouge2": 0.07816711590296496, "rougeL": 0.13440860215053763, "bert_precision": 0.6846293210983276, "bert_recall": 0.7211731672286987, "bert_f1": 0.7024262547492981, "gen_words": 520, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.48, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for a Grade II ankle sprain in a 22-year-old male basketball player. Clinical reasoning flows logically from mechanism to examination to diagnosis. However, several issues exist: (1) Special tests are misapplied - Tinel's test is a nerve percussion test, not used for ATFL; Weber's test classifies fibula fractures, not ligament sprains; the varus stress test being negative contradicts a positive anterior drawer with ATFL involvement. (2) The acute phase treatment is overly aggressive - manual therapy, ultrasound, TENS, and active exercises w", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4619, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 2, "title": "Addison's Disease", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Addison's Disease\n\n**Patient:** Mr. David Chen, 68-year-old male\n\n**Presenting Complaint:** Mr. Chen presented to physiotherapy with generalized fatigue, muscle weakness, weight loss, and a feeling of being \"run down\" for the past 6 months. He also reported experiencing occasional low blood pressure (hypotension) and dizziness, particularly upon standing (orthostatic hypotension). He had been diagnosed with Addison's disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) approximately 1 year ago.\n\n**History of Present Illness:**\nMr. Chen was diagnosed with Addison's disease following an adrenal crisis. He is currently on a lifelong hydrocortisone replacement therapy (cortisol) and an aldosterone replacement (fludrocortisone). He reports adherence to his medication regimen. He denies any recent changes in medication dosage or formulation. He reports difficulty sleeping and experiencing increased anxiety. He has a history of hypertension, which was well-controlled prior to his Addison's diagnosis. He also has a history of osteoarthritis in his knees, managed with over-the-counter analgesics. He is a retired accountant and spends most of his time at home.\n\n**Past Medical History:**\n*   Addison's disease (Primary Adrenal Insufficiency) - Diagnosed 1 year ago\n*   Hypertension (well-controlled)\n*   Osteoarthritis (knees)\n*   Mild GERD\n*   No history of diabetes, thyroid disorders, or autoimmune diseases other than Addison's.\n*   No history of smoking or significant alcohol use.\n\n**Social History:**\n*   Retired accountant.\n*   Lives alone.\n*   No significant support system outside of family.\n*   Reports limited mobility due to fatigue and pain.\n*   Reports difficulty managing his medications and appointments.\n\n**Family History:**\n*   Father died of a heart attack at age 65.\n*   Mother has type 2 diabetes.\n*   No known family history of autoimmune diseases.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n*   **General:** Appears fatigued, slightly cachectic, pale.\n*   **Vital Signs:** BP 95/60 mmHg (low), HR 90 bpm (slightly elevated), Temp 36.8\u00b0C (normal), RR 16 breaths/min (normal).\n*   **Cardiovascular:** Regular rhythm, no murmurs.\n*   **Respiratory:** Clear lungs.\n*   **Musculoskeletal:**\n    *   **General:** Diffuse muscle weakness, particularly in the proximal lower limbs.\n    *   **Neuromuscular:** Normal muscle tone, no fasciculations or tremors.\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Mildly restricted active and passive ROM in both knees due to pain and stiffness.\n    *   **Strength:** Mild proximal lower limb weakness (estimated 2/5 strength), distal lower limb strength preserved (3/5). Upper limb strength appears grossly normal (4/5).\n    *   **Balance:** Mild gait instability, particularly with eyes closed (positive Romberg sign).\n    *   **Gait:** Slow, shuffling gait with reduced step length.\n    *   **Posture:** Mild forward head posture, slight kyphosis.\n*   **Neurological:**\n    *   **Cranial Nerves:** Intact.\n    *   **Motor:** Mild proximal lower limb weakness as noted above.\n    *   **Sensory:** Intact.\n    *   **Reflexes:** Normal deep tendon reflexes.\n    *   **Coordination:** Slightly impaired, particularly with tandem walking.\n*   **Skin:** Pale, slightly dry.\n*   **Other:** No significant edema.\n\n**Investigations:**\n*   **Bloods:**\n    *   Electrolytes: Hyponatremia (125 mmol/L), Hyperkalemia (5.8 mmol/L), Hypoglycemia (4.5 mmol/L).\n    *   Renal function: Normal creatinine and BUN.\n    *   Adrenal function: Low morning cortisol, high ACTH (confirming primary adrenal insufficiency).\n*   **Imaging:** No relevant imaging performed.\n\n**Assessment:**\nMr. Chen presents with symptoms consistent with poorly controlled or inadequately managed Addison's disease, potentially exacerbated by the underlying condition and contributing to his current functional limitations. Key findings include:\n1.  **Generalized Fatigue and Muscle Weakness:** Likely multifactorial, potentially related to chronic illness, electrolyte imbalances (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia), hypoglycemia, low cortisol levels, and potentially deconditioning due to reduced activity. Proximal lower limb weakness is particularly noted.\n2.  **Orthostatic Hypotension:** A common symptom of adrenal insufficiency due to volume depletion and lack of mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) effect. This contributes to dizziness and reduced mobility.\n3.  **", "rouge1": 0.2923482849604222, "rouge2": 0.055995773903856315, "rougeL": 0.10448548812664907, "bert_precision": 0.7459390163421631, "bert_recall": 0.7519045472145081, "bert_f1": 0.7489099502563477, "gen_words": 575, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.32, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a well-structured clinical scenario for Addison's disease with appropriate history, physical examination, and investigations. Strengths include: accurate electrolyte findings (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia), appropriate medication context (hydrocortisone, fludrocortisone), relevant orthostatic hypotension discussion, and logical clinical reasoning. However, significant issues exist: (1) The case is truncated - the assessment cuts off mid-sentence and there is NO treatment plan provided, which severely impacts treatment_completeness. (2) Hypoglycemia is incorrectly listed a", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4122, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 119, "title": "Ankle Sprain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Ankle Sprain\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Name:** Mr. John Smith (fictional)\n*   **Age:** 34\n*   **Occupation:** Carpenter\n*   **Presenting Complaint:** Right ankle pain and swelling following a twisting injury during a work task.\n*   **Onset:** Acute, occurred 3 days ago.\n*   **Mechanism of Injury:** Tripped on an uneven surface while carrying a heavy tool, resulting in a twisting motion of the right ankle.\n*   **Symptoms:**\n    *   Pain, particularly on the lateral (outside) aspect of the ankle.\n    *   Swelling around the lateral ankle.\n    *   Tenderness to touch on the lateral malleolus (lateral ankle bone).\n    *   Difficulty bearing weight on the right leg.\n    *   Limited range of motion (ROM) in dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n    *   Feeling of instability or \"giving way\" in the ankle.\n*   **Previous History:** No significant prior history of ankle injuries. No history of osteoporosis or significant bone/joint disease.\n*   **Review of Systems:** No other significant symptoms reported (e.g., numbness, tingling, fever).\n\n**Initial Assessment (Day 1):**\n\n*   **Subjective:** Patient reports significant pain, swelling, and difficulty walking. States the ankle feels \"unstable\" and \"prone to rolling.\" Reports limited ability to perform work tasks requiring standing and walking.\n*   **Objective:**\n    *   **Inspection:** Moderate swelling and ecchymosis (bruising) present on the lateral aspect of the right ankle. No gross deformity.\n    *   **Palpation:** Significant tenderness over the lateral malleolus and along the lateral ligaments (likely ATFL and CFL).\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Marked limitation in active dorsiflexion (approximately 10 degrees) and plantarflexion (approximately 20 degrees). Pain reported with both movements. Passive ROM is similarly limited due to pain.\n    *   **Strength:** Gross weakness in plantarflexion and inversion/eversion due to pain and guarding. Difficulty with heel raises and toe raises.\n    *   **Neurological:** Intact sensation and motor function in the foot and lower leg. No signs of nerve compression.\n    *   **Functional:** Unable to bear weight through the right ankle (WBAT = 0/10). Difficulty with balance.\n*   **Assessment (Initial):** Based on the mechanism (twisting injury), location of pain/tenderness (lateral ankle), swelling, limited ROM, and inability to bear weight, the patient presents with a likely **Grade II Ankle Sprain** (subluxation/partial dislocation with ligament sprain). A Grade I sprain (ligament stretching without dislocation) is also possible, but the inability to bear weight suggests a higher grade. A Grade III sprain (complete dislocation) is less likely given the lack of gross deformity, but cannot be entirely ruled out without further assessment (e.g., drawer/screw test).\n\n**Treatment Plan (Initial Phase - Days 1-3):**\n\n*   **Goals:**\n    1.  Reduce pain and swelling.\n    2.  Protect the injured ligaments.\n    3.  Improve range of motion (ROM).\n    4.  Gradually improve strength and function.\n    5.  Educate the patient on injury prevention and management.\n*   **Interventions:**\n    *   **Modalities:**\n        *   **Ice:** Applied intermittently (15-20 minutes) multiple times per day to the lateral ankle to manage pain and swelling.\n        *   **Compression:** Elastic bandage applied to the lateral ankle to reduce swelling and provide support.\n        *   **Elevation:** Ankle kept elevated above the level of the heart whenever possible.\n    *   **Manual Therapy:**\n        *   **Soft Tissue Mobilization:** Gentle massage of the calf muscles (gastrocnemius, soleus) and peroneals to reduce muscle guarding and improve local circulation.\n        *   **Joint Mobilization:** Gentle, passive ROM exercises for the ankle joint (dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, inversion, eversion) performed within pain-free limits to maintain joint mobility.\n    *   **Exercise:**\n        *   **Isometric Exercises:** Gentle activation of ankle muscles (e.g., pressing foot into a ball, gentle inversion/eversion against resistance) performed as tolerated to promote neuromuscular activation without stressing the ligaments.\n        *   **Range of Motion (ROM) Exercises:** Gentle, pain-free passive and active-assisted ROM exercises (e.g., ankle alphabet, heel slides) to maintain flexibility.\n    *   **Functional Training:**\n        *   **Balance Exercises:** Simple balance exercises (e", "rouge1": 0.37229437229437223, "rouge2": 0.07225433526011561, "rougeL": 0.15151515151515152, "bert_precision": 0.7321633100509644, "bert_recall": 0.7601684331893921, "bert_f1": 0.7459030747413635, "gen_words": 598, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.45, "judge_overall": 0.52, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent clinical narrative for a lateral ankle sprain with appropriate mechanism, symptoms, and objective findings. Clinical reasoning from presentation to diagnosis is logical. However, there are notable issues: (1) The grading terminology is incorrect - Grade II is described as 'subluxation/partial dislocation' which is inaccurate; Grade II is partial ligament tear. Grade III is complete rupture, not 'complete dislocation.' (2) Ottawa Ankle Rules are not mentioned despite inability to bear weight, which is a significant omission for ruling out fracture. (3) Special", "clinical_msk_score": 0.5038, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 126, "title": "Osteoporosis & Fracture", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Osteoporosis & Fracture\n\n**Patient:** Mr. A.B. (Age: 72, Male)\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\nMr. A.B. presented to physiotherapy with a recent diagnosis of a fragility fracture (hip fracture) secondary to osteoporosis. He reported a sudden onset of severe left hip pain after a minor fall from standing height while reaching for a book on a high shelf. He denies significant trauma history prior to this fall. He also reported experiencing several \"minor\" falls over the past year without significant injury, which he attributed to aging.\n\n**Medical History:**\n*   **Diagnosis:** Osteoporosis (diagnosed 2 years ago based on DEXA scan showing T-score of -2.8 at the femoral neck).\n*   **Medications:** Alendronate (Bisphosphonate), Calcium and Vitamin D supplements.\n*   **Previous Fractures:** Denies previous fractures.\n*   **Other Medical Conditions:** Hypertension (controlled with medication), Hyperlipidemia (controlled with medication).\n*   **Past Surgical History:** Appendectomy.\n*   **Allergies:** Penicillin (rash).\n*   **Social History:** Retired accountant, lives alone in a single-story house, moderately active (walks 1-2 km daily), non-smoker, occasional alcohol.\n\n**Examination Findings:**\n\n*   **General:** Alert, oriented, appears tired but cooperative.\n*   **Musculoskeletal:**\n    *   **Left Hip:** Significant pain with passive range of motion (ROM) in all planes, particularly abduction and external rotation. Severe limitation in active ROM. Tenderness over the left greater trochanter and anterior hip region. Positive Trendelenburg sign on the left (indicating hip abductor weakness).\n    *   **Right Hip:** Full active and passive ROM. No pain or tenderness.\n    *   **Lower Limb:** Symmetrical muscle bulk. No significant edema or bruising. Normal gait pattern (although slightly altered due to left hip pain).\n    *   **Neurological:** Grossly intact motor function and sensation in lower limbs.\n*   **Functional Assessment:**\n    *   **Activities of Daily Living (ADLs):** Independent with basic ADLs (bathing, dressing, toileting) but requires assistance with transfers (bed to chair, chair to toilet) due to pain and fear of falling. Difficulty with stair climbing (requires handrails).\n    *   **Instrumental Activities of Daily Living (IADLs):** Difficulty with grocery shopping (carrying heavy bags), managing finances, and household chores. Requires assistance with these tasks.\n    *   **Balance:** Mildly impaired balance, particularly when standing on the left leg.\n    *   **Strength:** Significant weakness in left hip abductors (gluteus medius/minimus), quadriceps, and hamstrings. Mild weakness in right hip abductors.\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Limited active and passive ROM in the left hip (flexion 0-60\u00b0, abduction 0-20\u00b0, external rotation 0-15\u00b0). Right hip ROM is full.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n*   **Diagnosis:** Left hip fracture (likely femoral neck or intertrochanteric) secondary to osteoporosis.\n*   **Comorbidities:** Osteoporosis, age-related muscle weakness, mild balance impairment, functional limitations impacting IADLs.\n*   **Goals:**\n    1.  Pain management and reduction.\n    2.  Improve hip ROM and strength.\n    3.  Enhance balance and proprioception.\n    4.  Improve functional mobility and ADLs.\n    5.  Educate patient and family on osteoporosis management, fall prevention, and safe movement strategies.\n    6.  Promote patient confidence and reduce fear of falling.\n\n**Physiotherapy Plan:**\n\n**Phase 1: Acute Phase (First 2-4 weeks post-fracture)**\n\n*   **Goals:** Pain control, reduce inflammation, protect the fracture site, initiate gentle mobilization.\n*   **Interventions:**\n    *   **Pain Management:**\n        *   Ice application to the left hip (20 mins several times daily).\n        *   Analgesia (e.g., paracetamol, NSAIDs if appropriate and not contraindicated).\n        *   Heat application (once acute inflammation subsides) to promote relaxation.\n    *   **Mobilization:**\n        *   **Passive ROM:** Gentle passive ROM exercises for the left hip (flexion, abduction, external rotation) performed by the therapist, focusing on range without pain.\n        *   **Active-Assisted ROM:** Gradually introduce active-assisted ROM exercises for the left hip (e.g., seated abduction, assisted knee flexion/extension).\n    *   **Muscle Activation:**\n        *   **Isometrics:** Gentle isometrics for hip abductors (gl", "rouge1": 0.26854690374003676, "rouge2": 0.03314917127071823, "rougeL": 0.10545677498467199, "bert_precision": 0.7127177119255066, "bert_recall": 0.7536421418190002, "bert_f1": 0.7326088547706604, "gen_words": 575, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.75, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.75, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.55, "judge_overall": 0.55, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a clinically coherent narrative for a 72-year-old male with osteoporotic hip fracture. Clinical reasoning flows logically from history through examination to diagnosis and treatment planning. Diagnostic accuracy is reasonable though the case doesn't specify imaging confirmation or exact fracture classification, and special tests are limited. The treatment plan is organized by phases with some dosage detail (ice 20 mins), but the document is truncated mid-sentence during Phase 1 interventions, leaving treatment completeness significantly compromised - phases 2 and 3, pro", "clinical_msk_score": 0.5421, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 133, "title": "Calf muscle tear", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Calf Muscle Tear\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Name:** Mr. David Chen\n*   **Age:** 45\n*   **Occupation:** Construction Worker\n*   **Presenting Complaint:** Acute onset of severe pain, swelling, and inability to bear weight on his right leg following a fall while working on a ladder.\n*   **Date of Injury:** [Insert Date]\n*   **Date of First Physiotherapy Consultation:** [Insert Date]\n\n**History of Presenting Complaint:**\n\nMr. Chen reported falling from a ladder while performing roofing work. He landed heavily on his right knee, followed by a forceful kick-back motion with his right leg, resulting in immediate, severe pain in the posterior aspect of his right calf. He was unable to continue working and sought immediate medical attention.\n\n**Medical History:**\n\n*   No significant past medical history.\n*   No history of previous calf injuries or surgeries.\n*   No history of DVT or PE.\n*   No history of significant cardiovascular disease.\n*   No history of steroid use.\n*   No history of smoking or alcohol abuse.\n\n**Social History:**\n\n*   Lives alone.\n*   Maintains a physically active lifestyle, including weightlifting and occasional running.\n*   Works as a construction worker, requiring significant standing and walking.\n\n**Examination Findings:**\n\n*   **Observation:** Significant swelling and ecchymosis (bruising) noted in the posterior aspect of the right calf, extending from the mid-calf to the ankle. The calf muscle contour is markedly altered, appearing \"squared off\" or \"beaded\" due to the tear.\n*   **Palpation:** Severe tenderness to palpation over the gastrocnemius muscle belly, particularly along the medial and lateral heads. Pain elicited with passive stretching of the calf muscle.\n*   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Active knee flexion was severely limited due to pain. Passive knee flexion and extension were painful, especially in the mid-range. Ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion were painful, particularly dorsiflexion.\n*   **Strength Testing:**\n    *   **Gastrocnemius:** Significant weakness in both knee flexion (via the knee flexion portion of the muscle) and ankle plantarflexion (via the calf portion of the muscle). Estimated strength around 2/10 in both directions.\n    *   **Soleus:** Weakness noted in ankle plantarflexion (via the soleus muscle). Estimated strength around 3/10.\n    *   **Tibialis Anterior:** Strength testing was grossly limited by pain and swelling, but initial assessment suggests potential weakness in ankle dorsiflexion.\n*   **Special Tests:**\n    *   **Nilsson's Test:** Positive (painful and/or weak contraction of the gastrocnemius during passive ankle dorsiflexion).\n    *   **Noble's Test:** Positive (painful and/or weak contraction of the gastrocnemius during passive ankle plantarflexion).\n    *   **Kager's Test:** Positive (painful and/or weak contraction of the soleus during passive ankle dorsiflexion).\n    *   **Calf Squeeze Test:** Positive (inability to actively squeeze the calf muscle).\n*   **Neurological Examination:** Intact deep tendon reflexes (DTRs) in the lower limbs. No significant motor or sensory deficits noted.\n*   **Vascular Examination:** Pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) were intact and equal bilaterally. No signs of compartment syndrome (e.g., pain with passive stretch, paresthesias, pallor, pulselessness).\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\nBased on the history, clinical presentation, and examination findings, the diagnosis is a **complete tear of the right gastrocnemius muscle**, likely involving both the medial and lateral heads. The injury mechanism involved a forceful eccentric contraction or a direct impact. The severity is classified as **Grade III** (complete tear) based on the significant weakness and inability to actively contract the muscle.\n\n**Initial Management (Medical):**\n\n*   **Immobilisation:** The patient was initially immobilised with a posterior splint and crutches for pain relief and to prevent further injury.\n*   **Pain Management:** Analgesia was provided.\n*   **Elevation:** The right leg was elevated above the level of the heart to reduce swelling.\n*   **Ice:** Ice packs were applied intermittently to manage swelling and pain.\n*   **Compression:** Compression garments were applied to minimise swelling.\n*   **Referral:** The patient was referred to the physiotherapist for rehabilitation.\n\n**Physiotherapy Treatment Plan:**\n\n**Phase 1: Acute Phase (Weeks 0-4)**\n\n*   **Goal:** Reduce pain and swelling, protect the injured tissue, restore basic joint mobility", "rouge1": 0.3350694444444444, "rouge2": 0.06086956521739129, "rougeL": 0.15104166666666669, "bert_precision": 0.7029208540916443, "bert_recall": 0.7371292114257812, "bert_f1": 0.7196186780929565, "gen_words": 631, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.75, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.45, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a detailed calf muscle tear case with good clinical structure. Strengths include thorough history, vascular/neurological examination, red flag screening (DVT, compartment syndrome), and appropriate initial medical management. However, several issues reduce quality: (1) Special tests named (Nilsson's, Noble's, Kager's, Calf Squeeze Test) are largely fabricated or incorrectly described - Thompson's test is the standard for Achilles/calf assessment; (2) The mechanism (ladder fall with knee landing) is atypical for gastrocnemius tear - more commonly sudden push-off; (3) Str", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4565, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 140, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Name:** (Patient Name - Placeholder)\n*   **Age:** (Patient Age - Placeholder)\n   *   *Example: 6 months old*\n*   **Gender:** (Patient Gender - Placeholder)\n   *   *Example: Male*\n*   **Diagnosis:** Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV) - Clubfoot\n*   **Date of Initial Assessment:** (Date - Placeholder)\n*   **Date of Final Assessment:** (Date - Placeholder)\n*   **Duration of Treatment:** (Duration - Placeholder)\n   *   *Example: 6 months*\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\n\nThe patient presented with a diagnosis of Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot), characterized by the foot being abnormally turned inwards (equinus), downwards (plantar flexion), and outwards (varus). The presentation was noted during routine newborn screening/examination.\n\n**Medical History:**\n\n*   No significant past medical history relevant to the MSK system.\n*   No known family history of clubfoot or other significant musculoskeletal conditions.\n*   No previous injuries or surgeries.\n\n**Social History:**\n\n*   Parental report of normal pregnancy and delivery.\n*   No known environmental risk factors.\n*   Family support for physiotherapy treatment.\n\n**Physical Examination Findings (Initial Assessment):**\n\n*   **Inspection:**\n    *   Marked equinus deformity (plantar flexion).\n    *   Marked varus deformity (outward turning).\n    *   Marked cavus deformity (high arch).\n    *   Foot held in a \"clubfoot\" position (rocker-bottom appearance).\n    *   Skin appears intact, no signs of ulceration or significant swelling.\n*   **Palpation:**\n    *   Significant tightness in the gastrocnemius-soleus complex (calf muscles).\n    *   Tenderness over the Achilles tendon insertion.\n    *   Limited range of motion (ROM) in dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n    *   Limited ROM in abduction and adduction.\n*   **Active Range of Motion (AROM):**\n    *   **Dorsiflexion:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Plantarflexion:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Abduction:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Adduction:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n*   **Passive Range of Motion (PROM):**\n    *   **Dorsiflexion:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Plantarflexion:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Abduction:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n    *   **Adduction:** Severely limited (approximately 0-10 degrees).\n*   **Muscle Strength (Manual Muscle Testing - MMG):**\n    *   **Gastrocnemius:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Soleus:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Tibialis Anterior:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Extensor Hallucis Longus:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Flexor Hallucis Longus:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Tibialis Posterior:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Flexor Digitorum Longus:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   **Flexor Hallucis Brevis:** Weak (Grade 2/3)\n    *   *Note: Strength assessment is challenging in infants due to limited AROM and potential for guarding.*\n*   **Gait Analysis (if possible):**\n    *   Limited ability to ambulate independently.\n    *   If ambulating, gait pattern is characterized by significant toe-walking (equinus component), foot drop (due to dorsiflexion limitation), and compensatory movements.\n    *   High-arched gait (cavus).\n\n**Treatment Plan (Initial):**\n\nBased on the initial assessment, a comprehensive conservative management plan was initiated, focusing on serial casting and physiotherapy.\n\n1.  **Serial Casting:**\n    *   **Method:** Soft-shell serial casting was applied immediately following diagnosis.\n    *   **Frequency:** Casts were changed every 2-3 days.\n    *   **Goals:** To gradually correct the deformities (equinus, varus, cavus) and improve muscle length and ROM.\n    *   **Monitoring:** Regular monitoring of", "rouge1": 0.26638477801268495, "rouge2": 0.03177966101694915, "rougeL": 0.10359408033826639, "bert_precision": 0.6951794028282166, "bert_recall": 0.7493463754653931, "bert_f1": 0.7212473750114441, "gen_words": 478, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.3, "judge_overall": 0.28, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Manual muscle testing (MMT) grading applied to a 6-month-old infant is clinically inappropriate and could lead to harmful assessment practices. Gait analysis described for a non-ambulatory infant is misleading. 'Soft-shell serial casting' is non-standard and potentially unsafe compared to the eviden", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a clubfoot (CTEV) case with reasonable structure but several issues. Clinical coherence is adequate - it follows a logical flow from presentation to assessment to treatment, though the description of deformities contains an error (varus is inward turning, not outward). Diagnostic accuracy has issues: the deformity description is partially incorrect (equinus=plantarflexion is correct, but varus is inward not outward turning; cavus description is reasonable). ROM findings are oddly symmetric and don't reflect typical CTEV patterns. MMT grading in a 6-month-old infant is c", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3576, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 141, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Name:** Mr. A. B.\n*   **Age:** 6 months old\n*   **Gender:** Male\n*   **Diagnosis:** Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV) - Clubfoot\n*   **Date of Birth:** [Insert DOB]\n*   **Date of Initial Physiotherapy Assessment:** [Insert Date]\n*   **Referral Source:** [Insert Referring Doctor/Clinic]\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\n\nMr. A. B. presented for physiotherapy assessment at 6 months of age with a diagnosis of Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot). His mother reported that the condition was noted at birth and has been managed with serial casting. The current cast was due for removal.\n\n**History:**\n\n*   **Birth History:** Full-term birth, no complications.\n*   **Family History:** No known family history of clubfoot.\n*   **Previous Management:** Serial casting has been applied since birth, followed by stretching exercises.\n*   **Current Status:** The current cast was removed on [Insert Date]. The mother reports that the foot appears \"flat\" and \"curved\" and is concerned about the long-term implications. She is seeking physiotherapy to help correct the deformity and improve mobility.\n*   **Past Medical History:** Unremarkable.\n*   **Allergies:** None known.\n*   **Medications:** None.\n*   **Occupation:** [Insert Occupation]\n*   **Social History:** Lives with parents.\n\n**Assessment (Initial Physiotherapy Assessment - [Insert Date]):**\n\n*   **Observation:**\n    *   **Positioning:** The infant was positioned in a prone lying position.\n    *   **Foot Appearance:** The right foot was observed to be severely equinovarus (inverted), cavus (high arch), and adducted (turned inwards). The heel was plantarflexed (pointed downwards), the forefoot was dorsiflexed (pointed upwards), and the hindfoot was everted (turned outwards). The foot appeared tight and rigid.\n    *   **Gait (if applicable):** Not applicable at 6 months.\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):**\n        *   **Ankle:** Marked limitation in dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n        *   **Hindfoot:** Marked limitation in eversion and inversion.\n        *   **Midfoot:** Marked limitation in abduction and adduction.\n        *   **Forefoot:** Marked limitation in abduction and flexion.\n    *   **Muscle Tone:** Tightness observed in the gastrocnemius, soleus, tibialis posterior, tibialis anterior, peroneus longus, peroneus brevis, and plantar fascia. Some tightness in the anterior compartment muscles (e.g., tibialis anterior) was noted.\n    *   **Symmetry:** Significant asymmetry between the right and left foot.\n    *   **Skin:** Skin appeared intact, with no signs of breakdown or pressure sores over the cast.\n\n*   **Manual Therapy:**\n    *   **Plantar Fascia:** Gentle release techniques were attempted on the plantar fascia, with limited success due to the rigidity of the foot.\n    *   **Calf Muscles:** Gentle stretching was attempted on the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles, with limited effectiveness due to the tightness and cast constraint.\n    *   **Other:** No significant restrictions noted in other joints.\n\n*   **Functional Assessment:**\n    *   **Positioning:** The infant required significant assistance to maintain a neutral position.\n    *   **Mobility:** Limited ability to move the foot through its available range.\n    *   **Sensory:** No obvious sensory deficits observed during the assessment.\n\n*   **Outcome Measures (Initial):**\n    *   **Passive ROM:** (Estimated/Subjective)\n        *   Ankle Dorsiflexion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Ankle Plantarflexion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Hindfoot Eversion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Hindfoot Inversion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Midfoot Abduction: < 10 degrees\n        *   Midfoot Adduction: < 10 degrees\n        *   Forefoot Abduction: < 10 degrees\n        *   Forefoot Flexion: < 10 degrees\n    *   **Active ROM:** (Estimated/Subjective)\n        *   Ankle Dorsiflexion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Ankle Plantarflexion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Hindfoot Eversion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Hindfoot Inversion: < 10 degrees\n        *   Midfoot Abduction: < 10 degrees\n        ", "rouge1": 0.2874354561101549, "rouge2": 0.05172413793103448, "rougeL": 0.1290877796901893, "bert_precision": 0.6840935945510864, "bert_recall": 0.732026994228363, "bert_f1": 0.707249104976654, "gen_words": 540, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.15, "judge_overall": 0.18, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The case incorrectly describes the hindfoot as 'everted' and the forefoot as 'dorsiflexed' in clubfoot, which are opposite to the actual deformity (hindfoot varus, forefoot adduction/plantarflexion). Acting on these misdescriptions could lead to incorrect treatment direction. Additionally, no Ponset", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a 6-month-old with clubfoot (CTEV) managed with serial casting. Strengths: appropriate diagnosis, relevant history, and some clinical detail. Weaknesses: (1) The observation section contains a significant clinical error - it describes the hindfoot as 'everted' in clubfoot, when CTEV characteristically involves hindfoot varus/inversion. (2) The forefoot is described as 'dorsiflexed' which is incorrect - clubfoot involves forefoot adduction and cavus. (3) No Pirani or Dimeglio scoring used. (4) Treatment plan is essentially absent - no Ponseti method discussion, no bracin", "clinical_msk_score": 0.2699, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 142, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot (Talipes Equinovarus)\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Name:** (Patient Name - Placeholder)\n*   **Age:** (Patient Age - Placeholder)\n*   **Diagnosis:** Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot)\n*   **Date of Birth:** (Patient DOB - Placeholder)\n*   **Referral Source:** (e.g., Orthopaedic Surgeon, Paediatric Podiatrist, GP)\n*   **Date of Referral:** (Date - Placeholder)\n*   **Date of Initial Physiotherapy Assessment:** (Date - Placeholder)\n*   **Date of Final Assessment/Discharge:** (Date - Placeholder)\n*   **Physiotherapist:** (Your Name/Credentials)\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\n\nThe patient presents with a diagnosis of Clubfoot, characterized by the foot being turned inwards (equinus), downwards (varus), and inwards (inverted). This results in the foot being in a fixed, plantarflexed, inverted, and adducted position. The condition is congenital, meaning it was present at birth.\n\n**History of Presenting Complaint:**\n\n*   **Onset:** Present from birth.\n*   **Progression:** The condition was noted immediately after birth. The parents sought medical advice shortly thereafter.\n*   **Previous Interventions:** The patient has undergone serial casting (Ponseti method) treatment, which was successful in achieving a relatively straight foot. However, the patient has returned for physiotherapy due to persistent tightness, particularly in the calf muscles (gastrocnemius and soleus), Achilles tendon, and tibialis posterior. There may also be some residual positional tightness or weakness.\n*   **Impact on Function:** The patient is unable to stand flat-footed, walk independently without support, or participate fully in typical developmental activities like crawling, running, and playing. The condition may also impact comfort and potentially lead to secondary issues like hip or knee alignment problems if not addressed.\n*   **Family History:** (Mention if relevant, e.g., other family members with similar conditions, although clubfoot is primarily congenital).\n*   **Social History:** (Briefly mention family structure, support system if relevant).\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n*   **Subjective Assessment:**\n    *   Patient and parent interview: Discussed the history, previous treatment, current symptoms, functional limitations, and goals for physiotherapy.\n    *   Observation: Observed the patient's gait (or lack thereof), posture, and general movement patterns. Noted the position of the feet, particularly the degree of equinus (plantarflexion) and varus (inversion).\n    *   Functional Mobility Assessment: Assessed the ability to weight-bear, stand on toes, stand on heels, squat, and walk (if possible).\n    *   Pain Assessment: Assessed for any reported pain or discomfort related to the condition or physiotherapy interventions.\n*   **Objective Assessment:**\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):**\n        *   *Plantarflexion:* Significant limitation observed in both ankles, consistent with the equinus component of clubfoot.\n        *   *Dorsiflexion:* Limited, particularly in the right foot (if applicable), potentially contributing to the inversion.\n        *   *Inversion/Eversion:* Limited, particularly in the right foot (if applicable), consistent with the varus component.\n        *   *Hip/Knee ROM:* Assessed for any compensatory patterns or limitations.\n    *   **Muscle Strength:**\n        *   *Calf Muscles (Gastrocnemius/Soleus):* Significant tightness and reduced strength observed bilaterally, particularly in the gastrocnemius. This is a common finding in clubfoot due to the constant plantarflexion.\n        *   *Tibialis Posterior:* Assessed for strength and function, as it is a key muscle for inversion and dorsiflexion. May show weakness or tightness.\n        *   *Achilles Tendon:* Assessed for tightness and flexibility.\n        *   *Hip/Knee Muscles:* Assessed for strength and symmetry.\n    *   **Proprioception/Balance:** Assessed balance while standing on one leg, potentially with eyes closed. May show limitations due to altered foot position and muscle imbalances.\n    *   **Gait Analysis:** Observed the patient's gait pattern (or lack thereof). If walking, assessed for compensatory movements (e.g., excessive hip adduction, knee valgus).\n    *   **Postural Assessment:** Assessed general posture, looking for any compensatory changes due to the foot position.\n    *   **Special Tests:** (If applicable, e.g., assessing specific muscle tightness or joint restrictions).\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n*   Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot)\n*   Significant calf muscle (gastrocnemius) tightness and", "rouge1": 0.2866379310344828, "rouge2": 0.03023758099352052, "rougeL": 0.1228448275862069, "bert_precision": 0.703018069267273, "bert_recall": 0.7321926355361938, "bert_f1": 0.7173088192939758, "gen_words": 585, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.3, "judge_overall": 0.28, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study is truncated mid-sentence in the diagnosis section, with no treatment plan, goals, or outcome measures provided. The presenting complaint and history are reasonable for clubfoot/Ponseti method context. The assessment section is structured but generic, with placeholder text throughout and no specific measurements or values. ROM findings contain an error (plantarflexion described as 'significantly limited' when in clubfoot the issue is restricted dorsiflexion, not plantarflexion - the foot is already plantarflexed). The case lacks: specific ROM values, standardized outcome measure", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3587, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 143, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot\n\n**Patient:** John Doe (Pseudonym)\n\n**Age:** 18 months\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**\n*   Significant right foot deformity presenting at birth.\n*   Foot held in a plantarflexed (pointed downwards) and inverted position.\n*   Limited range of motion in the ankle and subtalar joints.\n*   Difficulty with walking, appearing \"toe-walking\" or \"scrunched-up\" gait.\n*   Parental concern regarding potential long-term impact on mobility and development.\n\n**History:**\n*   Born full-term via uncomplicated vaginal delivery.\n*   No significant family history of similar conditions.\n*   Initial presentation noted by parents at 6 months of age.\n*   Referral to physiotherapy due to persistent deformity and restricted mobility.\n*   Previous attempts at bracing (e.g., Ponseti method) may have been initiated but adherence or effectiveness unclear from parental report.\n\n**Clinical Examination:**\n*   **Observation:**\n    *   Significant plantarflexion of the right foot at the ankle joint.\n    *   Inversion of the right foot.\n    *   Forefoot adduction (toes pointing inwards).\n    *   Limited dorsiflexion of the right ankle (estimated <10 degrees).\n    *   Limited subtalar joint motion (inversion/eversion).\n    *   Slight equinus deformity (plantarflexion) of the ankle.\n    *   Appearance consistent with congenital talipes equinovarus (CTEV).\n*   **Palpation:**\n    *   Palpable tightness and contracture in the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles (calf muscles).\n    *   Palpable tightness in the Achilles tendon.\n    *   Palpable tightness in the tibialis posterior and flexor hallucis longus (common tight muscles in clubfoot).\n    *   Palpable tightness in the tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus (common tight muscles in clubfoot).\n*   **Range of Motion (ROM):**\n    *   **Ankle Dorsiflexion:** Marked limitation, estimated <10 degrees.\n    *   **Ankle Plantarflexion:** Marked limitation, estimated <10 degrees (though the foot is naturally in this position).\n    *   **Subtalar Joint:** Limited inversion and eversion.\n    *   **Metatarsophalangeal (MTP) Joints:** Limited flexion of the lesser toes (1st-4th).\n    *   **Interphalangeal (IP) Joints:** Limited flexion of the great toe (1st) and lesser toes.\n*   **Muscle Strength:**\n    *   Gross weakness observed in ankle dorsiflexion and inversion.\n    *   Gross weakness observed in ankle plantarflexion and eversion.\n    *   Weakness in intrinsic foot muscles.\n*   **Gait Analysis:**\n    *   Marked \"scrunched-up\" gait pattern.\n    *   Significant toe-walking on the right foot.\n    *   Foot drop observed during attempted weight-bearing on the right foot.\n    *   Increased pressure on the medial aspect of the right foot.\n    *   Limited stride length on the right side.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n*   Congenital Talipes Equinovarus (CTEV), commonly known as Clubfoot.\n\n**Treatment Goals:**\n1.  Improve ankle dorsiflexion.\n2.  Improve ankle plantarflexion (though natural position is plantarflexion, improving ROM is key).\n3.  Improve subtalar joint mobility (inversion/eversion).\n4.  Improve range of motion in the MTP and IP joints of the lesser toes.\n5.  Strengthen ankle dorsiflexors and invertors.\n6.  Strengthen intrinsic foot muscles.\n7.  Improve gait pattern and reduce compensatory mechanisms (toe-walking).\n8.  Promote functional independence and reduce risk of long-term complications (e.g., arthritis, gait abnormalities).\n\n**Physiotherapy Plan:**\n\n**Phase 1: Initial Management & Stretching (Weeks 1-4)**\n\n*   **Goal:** Gentle passive stretching of tight structures.\n*   **Interventions:**\n    *   **Passive Stretching:**\n        *   **Ankle Dorsiflexion:** Gentle passive dorsiflexion of the right ankle using a strap or towel. Hold stretch for 15-20 seconds, repeat 5 times, 2-3 times per day.\n        *   **Plantarflexion:** Gentle passive plantarflexion of the right ankle using a strap or towel. Hold stretch for 15-20 seconds, repeat 5 times, 2-3 times per day.\n        *   **Subtalar", "rouge1": 0.2422857142857143, "rouge2": 0.020618556701030924, "rougeL": 0.0982857142857143, "bert_precision": 0.7153048515319824, "bert_recall": 0.7313950657844543, "bert_f1": 0.7232604622840881, "gen_words": 530, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.38, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a clubfoot (CTEV) case in an 18-month-old with reasonable clinical structure. Strengths: clear diagnosis, logical flow from presentation to examination, appropriate identification of tight structures. Weaknesses: (1) History is inaccurate - clubfoot is typically identified at birth, not at 6 months; (2) The Ponseti method description is vague and incorrectly framed as 'bracing' rather than serial casting followed by bracing; (3) Palpation incorrectly lists tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus as 'common tight muscles' - these are typically weak/overpowered in ", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4275, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 144, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot (Talipes Equinovarus)\n\n**Patient Information:**\n\n*   **Age:** 6 months old male infant\n*   **Presenting Complaint:** Right foot presents with a fixed, inverted, and plantarflexed position. Mother reports difficulty with diaper changes and dressing the infant. Mother also notes the infant appears uncomfortable and may have limited range of motion in the affected foot.\n*   **History:** Full-term birth, no significant prenatal history. Normal birth weight and length. No previous episodes of similar symptoms. No family history of clubfoot.\n*   **Examination Findings:**\n    *   **Observation:** Right foot is markedly inverted (equinus), plantarflexed (varus), and adducted (cavo). The heel is prominent, and the toes are curled inwards. The foot appears rigid and fixed in this position.\n    *   **Palpation:** Firm, non-tender tissue palpable over the medial and lateral aspects of the foot, particularly around the Achilles tendon and midfoot.\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):** Limited active and passive ROM in all major joints of the right lower limb, especially the ankle (dorsiflexion, plantarflexion) and subtalar joint.\n    *   **Strength:** Grossly weak plantarflexion and dorsiflexion of the right ankle and foot.\n    *   **Gait:** Unable to stand or walk independently. Mother reports the infant walks with a \"rocking\" gait pattern, favouring the left leg.\n    *   **Neurovascular Assessment:** Intact sensation and motor function in the right lower limb. No signs of vascular compromise.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\nBased on the clinical presentation and examination findings, the diagnosis is **Right Clubfoot (Talipes Equinovarus)**. The specific type appears to be **Posterior Talocalcaneal Clubfoot (PTC)**, characterized by the hindfoot being fixed in equinus (plantarflexion) and the calcaneus fixed in varus (inversion).\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\nThe primary goal of physiotherapy is to improve the foot's position, restore range of motion, strengthen muscles, and facilitate independent ambulation. The treatment plan will be implemented in conjunction with orthopaedic management (e.g., casting, bracing, surgery).\n\n**Phase 1: Initial Management & Positioning (Weeks 0-4)**\n\n*   **Goal:** Maintain the foot in a neutral position, prevent contracture progression, and encourage gentle passive movement.\n*   **Interventions:**\n    *   **Positioning:** Frequent repositioning of the infant during sleep and feeding. The infant should be positioned lying on their back with the affected right leg positioned in a neutral or slightly dorsiflexed position, supported by pillows under the hips and knees. Avoid positioning the affected leg in plantarflexion or inversion.\n    *   **Passive Stretching:** Gentle, passive stretching of the gastrocnemius-soleus complex (calf muscles) and tibialis anterior (shin muscles) may be attempted *only* if the infant is relaxed and cooperative. This is typically done by the parent or caregiver, focusing on gentle dorsiflexion and inversion. *Caution: Avoid forceful stretching, especially in the early stages.*\n    *   **Massage:** Gentle massage of the calf muscles and dorsum of the foot to encourage relaxation and improve tissue mobility.\n    *   **Observation:** Close monitoring for any signs of discomfort, redness, or skin breakdown.\n\n**Phase 2: Active ROM & Strengthening (Weeks 4-12)**\n\n*   **Goal:** Gradually improve active range of motion, strengthen muscles supporting the foot, and introduce weight-bearing activities.\n*   **Interventions:**\n    *   **Active ROM:** Encourage active dorsiflexion and inversion exercises. Use visual cues (e.g., pointing the toes towards the parent) and provide gentle resistance if possible.\n    *   **Strengthening:** Introduce targeted exercises for the tibialis anterior (inversion), gastrocnemius-soleus (dorsiflexion), and peroneus longus/brevis (eversion). Start with isometric contractions (muscle contraction without joint movement) and progress to isotonic exercises (using resistance).\n    *   **Weight-Bearing:** Introduce gentle weight-bearing activities, such as supervised time in a crib or playpen with the affected leg positioned in a neutral or slightly dorsiflexed position. Ensure the infant is comfortable and not excessively stressed.\n    *   **Gait Training:** Encourage the infant to take steps, initially with support (e.g., holding hands) and gradually reducing support as strength and ROM improve. Focus on encouraging a neutral gait pattern.\n    *   **Hydrotherapy:** Gentle hydrotherapy sessions may be beneficial for improving ROM and strengthening, as the buoyancy of water can reduce joint resistance.\n\n**Phase 3: Functional Integration & Gait Training (Months", "rouge1": 0.3227990970654628, "rouge2": 0.04632768361581921, "rougeL": 0.1275395033860045, "bert_precision": 0.7309948801994324, "bert_recall": 0.7569388747215271, "bert_f1": 0.7437406778335571, "gen_words": 641, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.25, "judge_overall": 0.25, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The case recommends passive stretching into inversion for a clubfoot patient, when eversion/abduction/dorsiflexion are the correct directions. It also describes a 6-month-old walking with a gait pattern, which is developmentally impossible and could mislead clinicians. Strengthening exercises prescr", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a 6-month-old with clubfoot but contains several clinical errors and inconsistencies. Key issues: (1) The terminology is confused - equinus refers to plantarflexion, not inversion; varus is inversion, not plantarflexion; cavus is the arch deformity - these are mislabeled in the observation section. (2) A 6-month-old cannot walk, yet the case mentions gait assessment and mother reporting a 'rocking gait' - this is clinically incoherent. (3) The gold standard treatment (Ponseti method with serial casting) is not adequately described - physiotherapy alone is not primary ma", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3365, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0b_base_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 145, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Physiotherapy Case Study: Clubfoot (Talipes Equinovarus)\n\n**Patient:** Mr. A.B., 6-month-old male infant\n\n**Referral:** Referred by pediatric orthopaedic surgeon due to suspected clubfoot (Talipes Equinovarus - TE).\n\n**Presenting Complaint:** Mother reports difficulty with dressing Mr. A.B.'s feet and notes that his feet appear \"turned inward\" and \"pointed downward.\" She also mentions a history of a similar condition in her own foot (first metatarsal fracture) and expresses concern about the potential impact on his development and future mobility.\n\n**History of Present Illness:**\n* **Onset:** Noted by mother during routine infant check-up at 3 months old.\n* **Symptoms:** Feet are consistently in a plantarflexed (pointed downward), inverted (turned inward) position. Limited dorsiflexion (ability to pull foot upwards towards toes). Difficulty with passive dorsiflexion and inversion. No reported pain, swelling, or redness.\n* **Previous Management:** Mother has been applying a simple sock stretch method at home, but this has not resulted in significant improvement.\n* **Family History:** Mother has a history of a first metatarsal fracture, suggesting a potential predisposition to foot deformities.\n\n**Past Medical History:** Unremarkable. No history of previous significant injuries or surgeries.\n\n**Social History:** Lives with mother and father. Both parents are employed full-time. No known allergies.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n\n* **General:** Alert and interactive infant.\n* **Vitals:** Stable.\n* **Musculoskeletal:**\n    * **Inspection:** Significant bilateral clubfoot deformity. The right foot is more severely affected than the left. The foot is markedly plantarflexed, inverted, and adducted (turned inward). The heel is prominent, and the forefoot is pointed downward. There is a noticeable contracture of the Achilles tendon (equinus) and the posterior tibialis muscle (inverted). The foot is held in a \"rocker-bottom\" appearance.\n    * **Palpation:** Mild tenderness over the Achilles tendon insertion. No significant swelling, warmth, or crepitus.\n    * **Range of Motion (ROM):**\n        * **Dorsiflexion:** Markedly limited active dorsiflexion (approx. 10-15 degrees). Passive dorsiflexion is also significantly limited (approx. 20-25 degrees).\n        * **Inversion:** Limited active inversion (approx. 15-20 degrees). Passive inversion is also limited (approx. 25-30 degrees).\n        * **Plantarflexion:** Full active and passive plantarflexion.\n        * **Adduction:** Limited active and passive adduction.\n    * **Strength:** Grossly limited active ROM due to contractures. Passive ROM testing reveals the limited range. Muscle strength testing is difficult due to the limited active ROM, but passive resistance testing suggests underlying muscle weakness, particularly in the tibialis anterior and peroneus longus/brevis.\n    * **Sensation/Proprioception:** Within normal limits.\n    * **Neurological:** Cranial nerves and peripheral nerve function appear intact.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\n* **Diagnosis:** Bilateral Talipes Equinovarus (Clubfoot). The right foot is more severely affected than the left.\n* **Pathophysiology:** Clubfoot is a congenital deformity resulting from the intrauterine positioning of the fetus in a non-stressful position (often breech), followed by a failure of the neuromuscular system to correct the deformity postnatally. This leads to progressive tightening of the ligaments, tendons, and muscles, resulting in the characteristic plantarflexion, inversion, and adduction.\n* **Severity:** Moderate to severe, given the significant limitations in active and passive ROM, particularly dorsiflexion and inversion. The right foot appears more severely affected.\n* **Potential Complications:** If left untreated, clubfoot can lead to:\n    * **Pain:** Chronic pain due to abnormal joint mechanics and altered gait.\n    * **Functional Impairment:** Difficulty walking, running, and participating in activities.\n    * **Joint Damage:** Increased risk of osteoarthritis and joint instability later in life.\n    * **Psychosocial Impact:** Potential impact on self-esteem and social interactions.\n\n**Treatment Plan:**\n\nThe primary treatment for clubfoot is the Ponseti method, a gentle, non-surgical technique involving serial casting and manipulation. Physiotherapy plays a crucial role in supporting this method and facilitating recovery.\n\n**Phase 1: Initial Assessment and Preparation (Weeks 1-2)**\n\n* **Goal:** Assess the severity and extent of the deformity, establish rapport with the patient and family, explain the treatment plan, and prepare the patient for the first cast.\n* **Interventions:**\n    * **Observation:** Continue monitoring the infant's feet and general condition", "rouge1": 0.3288461538461539, "rouge2": 0.04624277456647398, "rougeL": 0.125, "bert_precision": 0.7160519361495972, "bert_recall": 0.7531547546386719, "bert_f1": 0.7341348528862, "gen_words": 627, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.3, "judge_overall": 0.35, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The case incorrectly describes a 'rocker-bottom' foot appearance as a feature of clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). Rocker-bottom foot is characteristic of congenital vertical talus, a different and opposing deformity. This mischaracterization could lead to diagnostic confusion and inappropriate treatm", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a clubfoot case with reasonable structure but contains several clinical inaccuracies and issues. The pathophysiology description is oversimplified and partially incorrect (breech positioning is a risk factor, not the primary cause). The family history conflation of a metatarsal fracture with hereditary foot deformity is clinically misleading. The ROM values described are internally inconsistent (active dorsiflexion of 10-15\u00b0 is actually reasonable, not markedly limited for clubfoot). The 'rocker-bottom' description is incorrect for clubfoot (it describes a different def", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3868, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A1_distill_only", "seed": 0, "case_id": 2, "title": "Addison's Disease", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Case Study: Unstilling of Intra-articular Levers in Addison's Disease: A Collaborative Approach to Functional Rehabilitation\n\n**Introduction**\n\nAddison's Disease (AD) is a complex and heterogeneous condition characterized by severe, progressive hip and lumbarvertebral dysfunction. The exact etiology remains unclear, but potential contributors include bone disease, cartilage disease, and systemic inflammation.  This case study explores the collaborative approach to functional rehabilitation following a history of left-sided hip arthroplasia (LHA) and lumbarvertebrae dysfunction.  The goal was to address the combined clinical deficits of the condition, specifically the functional limitations in the hip and lumbarvertebrae, which were subsequently ameliorated through therapeutic interventions.\n\n**Case Overview**\n\n*   **Patient Details:**\n    *   **Name:**  [Insert Patient Name Here]\n    *   **Age:** 62\n    *   **Diagnosis:**  Severe hip arthroplasia (LHA) with subclinical lumbarvertebral dysfunction.\n    *   **Chief Complaint:**  Limited range of motion in the upper extremity (e.g., right arm).\n    *   **History of Previous Surgery:**  Left-sided hip arthroplasia.\n    *   **Primary Diagnoses:**  Weight-bearing restrictions, reduced range of motion, and hip pain.\n'\n\n*   **Physical Assessment:**\n\n    *   **Right Arm:**  Assessment of muscle strength, range of motion, and activity level.  Assessment of hip pain, tenderness, and range of motion deficits were performed.\n    *   **Left Arm:**  Assessment of  (LHA) range of motion, range of activity, and activity level.  Assessment of  (LHA)  left-sided hip pain and pain-free range of motion.  Assessment of left-sided hip pain and pain-free range of motion.\n    *   **Hip (LHA):**  Assessment of hip flexion, extension, and range of motion.  Assessment of hip flexion, extension, and range of motion deficits.\n    *   **Lumbar (LHA):** Assessment of lumbar flexion, extension, and range of motion.  Assessment of lumbar flexion, extension, and range of motion deficits.\n\n    *   **Assessment of Left-Side Hip (LHA) with Symptoms:**\n        *   **Range of Motion Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA)  left-sided hip  range of motion deficits.  Note:  This is a simplified summary.  A full assessment would involve  (LHA)  range of motion assessment, including  (LHA)  left-sided hip  range of motion assessments.  The assessment was performed on a  (LHA)  left-sided hip (LHA)  range of motion assessment.\n-   **Assessment of Left-Side Hip (LHA) with Symptoms:**\n        *   **Hip Flexion/Extension Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip flexion/extension  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip  flexion/extension assessment.\n        *   **Hip Extension/Rotation Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  extension/rotation  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip  extension/rotation assessment.\n        *   **Hip Range of Motion Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  range of motion assessment.\n        *   **Hip flexion/extension Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  flexion/extension  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip  flexion/extension assessment.\n        *   **Hip rotation assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  rotation  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip  rotation assessment.\n        *   **Hip flexion/extension Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  flexion/extension  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip  flexion/extension assessment.\n-   **Assessment of Left-Side Hip (LHA) with Symptoms:**\n        *   **Hip Pain Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip pain  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip pain assessment.\n        *   **Hip  Pain Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  pain  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip pain assessment.\n        *   **Hip  Pain Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  pain  (LHA)  left-sided hip  hip pain assessment.\n        *   **Hip  Pain Assessment:**  Assessment of  (LHA) left-sided hip  hip  pain", "rouge1": 0.14308768154922, "rouge2": 0.01830910070005385, "rougeL": 0.07853684776761699, "bert_precision": 0.708459198474884, "bert_recall": 0.698188066482544, "bert_f1": 0.7032861709594727, "gen_words": 503, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.0, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.0, "judge_overall": 0.02, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "Addison's Disease is a life-threatening endocrine condition requiring medical management including corticosteroid replacement. The case study completely misrepresents it as a musculoskeletal hip condition, which could mislead clinicians into missing critical red flags such as adrenal crisis, electro", "judge_reasoning": "This case study is fundamentally flawed on multiple levels. First, Addison's Disease is a primary adrenal insufficiency (endocrine condition), not a musculoskeletal condition involving hip arthroplasia or lumbarvertebral dysfunction - the introduction completely misrepresents the condition. The term 'hip arthroplasia' is not standard medical terminology. The case study is highly repetitive, with assessment sections copy-pasted multiple times with minimal variation. There are no actual clinical findings, no objective measurements, no special tests, no differential diagnosis, no treatment plan, ", "clinical_msk_score": 0.1555, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A1_distill_only", "seed": 0, "case_id": 119, "title": "Ankle Sprain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Case Study: Ankle Sprain - Trainee Case\n\n**Client Name:** Amelia Silva\n\n**Client Profile (Assessment & Diagnosis):**\n\n*   **Age:** 32\n*   **Gender:** Female\n*   **Height:** 165cm\n*   **Weight:** 160kg\n*   **Occupation:** Part-time Graphic Designer\n\n**Diagnosis (Assessment & Treatment):**\n\nAmelia's ankle sprain was described as a recent acute injury, suggesting a progressive condition. The initial assessment was focused on the bilateral nature of the injury.\n\n*   **Assessment:** The patient was diagnosed with a partial ankle sprain in the anterior-median area. This injury was not a complete sprain, but a partial anterior-median partial sprain. The key characteristics were:\n    *   **Initial Pain:** Pain was described as sharp and dull, with a distinct onset at the beginning of the injury. Pain was noted on palpation of the ankle joint and the anterior-median area.\n    *   **Joint Stiffness:** Stiffness was noted in the anterior-median area, suggesting a characteristic gait pattern.\n    *   **Timing:** The injury was felt within 15-20 minutes of the onset of pain.\n\n*   **Physical Therapy:** After initial assessment, the following initial physical therapy interventions were performed.\n\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM) Exercises:** The patient was instructed to perform a range of motion exercises to improve ankle flexibility and range of motion in the anterior-median area. The initial exercises were:\n        *   **Ankle Dorsiflexion (Ankle-to-Ankle):** A full-body, 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Belly:** A 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Shoulder:** A 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Wrist:** A 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Midfoot:** A 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Hip:** A 5-10-10 bilateral exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n    *   **Isometric Exercises:** Isometric exercises were performed to help increase the range of motion in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Ankle:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Belly:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Shoulder:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Wrist:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Midfoot:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Hip:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n    *   **Proprioception Exercises:** Exercises to improve proprioception (awareness of body position) were also introduced.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Ankle:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Belly:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Shoulder:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Wrist:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Midfoot:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Hip:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Shoulder:** A 15-20-10 isometric exercise performed in the anterior-median area.\n        *   **Ankle-to-Wrist:** A 15-20-1", "rouge1": 0.2825112107623318, "rouge2": 0.040419161676646706, "rougeL": 0.1554559043348281, "bert_precision": 0.7043843269348145, "ber