{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A1_distill_only", "seed": 0, "case_id": 141, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Case Study: Rehabilitation of Clubfoot in a Child with Hypertension\n\n**Introduction:**\n\nClubfoot, a chronic neurological condition characterized by the characteristic foot-to-toe movement of the foot, is a common manifestation of hypertension. This case study focuses on the rehabilitation of a young, healthy boy experiencing clubfoot due to hypertension, highlighting the importance of early intervention and the potential benefits of multidisciplinary physiotherapy interventions.\n\n**Case Details:**\n\n*   **Case Name:** Alex (Child)\n\n*   **Age:** 10 years (Height: 152cm, Weight: 160kg)\n\n*   **Diagnosis:** Clubfoot (Clubfoot was initially reported in the early 2000s, but it has been managed with medication and physiotherapy and is now treated with a combination of diet and exercise.)\n\n*   **History of Hypertension (Age):** Alex is currently a registered antihypertensive (ACE-Ibuprofen) patient. He has had hypertension for 10 years, and he is currently on medication to manage his hypertension. He has also been advised to be able to be held and supported by his doctor.\n\n*   **Conditioning:** Alex has been advised to follow a 20-hour daily walk, and he has been advised to work with his doctor to prevent the onset of clubfoot and to manage the associated symptoms.\n\n*   **Physical Examination:** Alex was noted to be experiencing a characteristic clubfoot. The clubfoot was more prominent at the base of the foot than the adjacent toes. He could also show signs of pain or stiffness on his left foot, which was not typically present on his left leg.\n\n  *   Alex showed signs of weakness and fatigue at the foot and leg.\n\n*   **Assessment and Treatment Plan:**\n\n    *   **Assessment:** Alex was assessed by his parents to assess his overall health and identify any potential underlying conditions. The parents were aware of the condition and encouraged Alex to manage his symptoms.\n    *   **Treatment Plan:**\n        *   **Dietary Management:** Alex was instructed to adhere to a low-sodium diet (dietary management) to prevent complications. The diet was tailored to his individual needs and was carefully monitored.\n        *   **Physical Therapy:** The patient was prescribed a combination of the following physical therapy techniques:\n            *   **Epley Exercises:** These exercises are used to improve foot flexibility, gait, and balance. Alex was instructed to do these exercises at least 3 times per week.\n            *   **Foot and Leg Massage:** The patient was instructed to do foot and leg massage sessions 2-3 times per week.\n            *   **Orthotics:** The patient was encouraged to use orthotics such as a custom-fitted gait trainer to help with foot and leg movement.\n            *   **Orthotics:** The patient was instructed to use orthotics such as a custom-fitted gait trainer to help with foot and leg movement.\n    *   **Patient Education:** Alex was educated on the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and exercise habits to prevent complications from clubfoot. He was also educated on the benefits of a low-sodium diet and the importance of a balanced lifestyle.\n\n    *   **Follow-up:** After 12 weeks of treatment, Alex was followed up with his parents every 3-6 weeks to monitor his progress and adjust the treatment plan as needed. The following follow-up was encouraged to the following year.\n\n**Clinical Considerations:**\n\n*   **Physical Examination:** The physical examination revealed no signs of complications.\n*   **Functional Assessment:** The patient was assessed for functional impairment (e.g., difficulty walking, balance problems) with a gait analysis and balance assessment.\n*   **Assessment of the Clubfoot:** The patient was assessed to determine the extent of clubfoot.\n*   **Treatment Plan:** The patient was instructed to follow the prescribed physical therapy treatment plan.\n\n**Case Outcome (Children with Hypertension):**\n\n*   **Impact on the Patient:** The patient's clubfoot is significantly reduced. It is not only a visible problem but also a side effect of the management of hypertension.\n*   **Patient Benefits:** The patient is now able to walk with confidence and comfort. He is able to enjoy the activities he has always wanted to do. He is able to enjoy the activities he has always wanted to do.\n*   **Patient Education:** The patient was educated on the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and exercise habits to prevent complications from clubfoot.\n*   **Patient Education:** The patient was educated on the importance of maintaining a healthy weight and exercise habits to prevent complications from clubfoot.\n*   **Collaboration with Family:** The patient's parents were actively involved in the care of the patient. The parents were encouraged to discuss their own health and to participate in the care of the patient. The parents were encouraged to participate in the care of the patient.\n\n**Advantages of Case Study:**\n\n*", "rouge1": 0.3988644428672818, "rouge2": 0.07391613361762615, "rougeL": 0.16465578424414481, "bert_precision": 0.7229266166687012, "bert_recall": 0.7362480163574219, "bert_f1": 0.7295264601707458, "gen_words": 739, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.0, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.0, "judge_overall": 0.03, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The case recommends a '20-hour daily walk' which is physically impossible and dangerous for any patient, especially a child. It prescribes 'ACE-Ibuprofen' which is not a real medication. Epley exercises are contraindicated/irrelevant for clubfoot. The fundamental mischaracterization of clubfoot as a", "judge_reasoning": "This case study contains numerous critical errors. Clubfoot is described as a 'chronic neurological condition' caused by hypertension - both completely wrong (it's a congenital musculoskeletal deformity). The patient demographics are implausible (10-year-old weighing 160kg). 'ACE-Ibuprofen' is not a real medication. A '20-hour daily walk' is dangerous and nonsensical. Epley maneuvers are for BPPV (vestibular condition), not clubfoot. The case lacks any evidence-based clubfoot management (Ponseti method, serial casting, bracing). The physical examination description is vague and incoherent. The", "clinical_msk_score": 0.1729, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A1_distill_only", "seed": 0, "case_id": 142, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "## Case Study: Clubfoot - A Presentation of Patient and Initial Assessment\n\n**Patient Name:** Sarah Johnson\n\n**Age:** 65\n\n**Diagnosis:** Clubfoot (Clubfoot - Condition)\n\n**Case Summary:**\n\nSarah Johnson is a 65-year-old female, with a history of developing clubfoot from a childhood injury. She has a history of arthritis in the lower extremities. She presents with a constellation of symptoms including a history of clubfoot, decreased range of motion, and a vague feeling of discomfort in her upper extremities.\n\n**Detailed Assessment:**\n\n*   **History of Injury:** Sarah was admitted to the clinic for a type 1 upper extremity clubfoot (A-XII) due to a childhood injury of a small, displaced ankle. This injury was then aggravated by a minor, intermittent calf calf pain. While not a major injury, it is a likely contributing factor to the clinical presentation.\n\n*   **Clinical Presentation:**\n\n    *   **General Appearance:** Sarah has a characteristic, low-set, thin-to-medium-thick face with a slight asymmetry in her upper lip. Her upper extremities are markedly less defined than her lower extremities. She has a slightly wider, more defined upper lip. Her upper leg has a slightly more defined lower leg than her lower leg. She has a small, small, slightly thickened upper and lower lip, a small, small, slightly thickened upper and lower lip. She has a thin, thin-to-medium-thick upper and lower lip.\n    *   **Range of Motion (ROM):** The range of motion in her upper extremities is significantly reduced compared to her lower extremities. Her upper extremities have reduced range of motion from 20-30% to 10-15% of her lower extremities. Her lower extremities have a range of motion of 10-15% to 20% of her lower extremities. This suggests a degree of muscle weakness in her upper and lower extremities.\n    *   **Pain and Tendernes