3505249023438, "bert_f1": 0.7454448342323303, "gen_words": 888, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.0, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.0, "judge_overall": 0.02, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "This case study is severely deficient across all dimensions. Clinically, clubfoot (talipes equinovarus) is a congenital condition typically diagnosed at birth, not acquired in a 45-year-old male from 'a recent injury.' The etiology presented is clinically inaccurate. The case contains massive repetition of the same paragraph (~16+ times) with no actual examination findings, no objective measures, no special tests, no differential diagnosis, no treatment plan, no physiotherapy interventions, no dosage or progression, and no functional goals. There is no clinical reasoning whatsoever. The conten", "clinical_msk_score": 0.1678, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 2, "title": "Addison's Disease", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Addison's Disease\n\nIntroduction\n\nAddison's disease, also known as primary adrenal insufficiency, is a rare endocrine disorder characterized by insufficient production of cortisol and aldosterone by the adrenal glands. Cortisol is a steroid hormone that helps regulate metabolism, blood pressure, and inflammation, while aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid that regulates blood pressure and electrolyte balance. Cortisol deficiency leads to fatigue, weight loss, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, muscle and joint pain, and hypoglycemia. Aldosterone deficiency leads to hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and volume depletion. Addison's disease can be caused by autoimmune destruction of the adrenal glands (most common), infections, hemorrhage, metastatic cancer, or congenital adrenal hyperplasia.\n\nThis case study will focus on a 68-year-old male patient presenting with fatigue, muscle and joint pain, weight loss, and electrolyte abnormalities suggestive of Addison's disease. The patient will undergo physical examination, laboratory testing, and imaging studies to confirm the diagnosis. Treatment with hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone will be initiated, followed by physical therapy interventions to address the patient's functional limitations and improve quality of life.\n\nClinical Presentation\n\nA 68-year-old male patient with a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with a 3-month history of progressive fatigue, muscle weakness, and joint pain. The patient reports experiencing nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain, particularly in the morning. He has also experienced unexplained weight loss and orthostatic hypotension, characterized by dizziness upon standing. He denies any fever, cough, or shortness of breath. He reports increased thirst and urination.\n\nPhysical Examination\n\nOn examination, the patient is found to be alert and oriented, but appears fatigued. His blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg, and his heart rate is 85 bpm. His weight is 80 kg, and his height is 175 cm, resulting in a BMI of 23.5 kg/m\u00b2. He is afebrile. His skin is pale and dry. His abdominal examination is unremarkable, except for mild epigastric tenderness. His gait is unsteady, and he has difficulty rising from a chair. He reports mild pain in his shoulders, hips, and knees. He denies any urinary or bowel incontinence.\n\nLaboratory Findings\n\nBlood tests reveal a low serum sodium level (125 mmol/L), a high serum potassium level (6.1 mmol/L), and a low serum bicarbonate level (18 mmol/L). His serum cortisol level is low (1.5 mcg/dL), and his ACTH level is high (150 pg/mL). His serum glucose level is 100 mg/dL.\n\nImaging Studies\n\nAn abdominal CT scan shows a small, calcified adrenal gland.\n\nDiagnosis\n\nBased on the clinical presentation, laboratory findings, and imaging studies, the patient is diagnosed with Addison's disease.\n\nTreatment\n\nHydrocortisone 20 mg daily and fludrocortisone 0.05 mg daily are prescribed. The patient is instructed to take hydrocortisone with food and to monitor his blood glucose levels.\n\nPhysical Therapy Interventions\n\nThe patient is referred to physical therapy. During the initial assessment, it is noted that he has decreased strength in his lower extremities, poor balance, and difficulty with transfers. He also has poor muscle endurance and increased pain with prolonged standing.\n\nInterventions\n\nThe physical therapist will begin with gentle range-of-motion exercises to improve joint mobility and reduce pain. They will incorporate strengthening exercises for the lower extremities, focusing on quadriceps, hamstrings, and gluteal muscles. Balance training exercises, such as heel-toe walking and tandem stance, will be introduced to improve stability and reduce the risk of falls. The physical therapist will also implement aerobic conditioning exercises, such as walking, to improve cardiovascular endurance and overall fitness. Additionally, the physical therapist will educate the patient on proper body mechanics and provide recommendations for home exercise programs to maintain functional independence.\n\nOutcome\n\nThe patient demonstrates significant improvement in strength, balance, and endurance within 8 weeks of physical therapy. His pain levels decrease, and he is able to rise from a chair with less difficulty. He also experiences decreased orthostatic hypotension. His quality of life improves, and he is able to return to his previous level of activity.\n\nConclusion\n\nAddison's disease can cause significant functional limitations, including fatigue, muscle weakness, and pain. Physical therapy interventions, such as range-of-motion exercises, strengthening exercises, balance training, and aerobic conditioning, can help to improve strength, balance, and endurance, and improve overall quality of life. Physical therapy is an important part of the multidisciplinary management of Addison's disease.", "rouge1": 0.414161008729389, "rouge2": 0.10679611650485435, "rougeL": 0.15518913676042675, "bert_precision": 0.8000048398971558, "bert_recall": 0.753825306892395, "bert_f1": 0.7762288451194763, "gen_words": 695, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.75, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.5, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.45, "judge_overall": 0.48, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The physiotherapy section recommends aerobic conditioning and strengthening exercises without any mention of adrenal crisis risk during physical exertion, the need for stress dosing of hydrocortisone before intense exercise, or warning signs that should prompt cessation of exercise. This omission co", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a coherent narrative from presentation to treatment for Addison's disease. Clinical reasoning is logical: symptoms, labs (low cortisol, high ACTH, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia), imaging, and diagnosis align well. However, Addison's disease is primarily an endocrine condition, not an MSK condition, which affects clinical relevance and diagnostic accuracy scores. The physiotherapy interventions lack specificity: no dosage (sets/reps/frequency/duration/intensity), no clear progression criteria, and no mention of adrenal crisis precautions during exercise (a significant safet", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4963, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 119, "title": "Ankle Sprain", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Ankle Sprain\n\nPatient: Mr. A, 65-year-old male\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n\nMr. A is a retired construction worker who presented to physiotherapy clinic with acute right ankle pain and swelling. He fell while gardening, twisting his right ankle on a rock. He reports feeling a \"pop\" at the time of injury and was unable to bear weight immediately. He has experienced pain, swelling, and stiffness in his right ankle since the injury. He also reports difficulty with his usual daily activities, such as walking, climbing stairs, and getting in and out of his car. He reports the pain is worse with activity and at night. He has no prior history of ankle injuries.\n\nPhysical Examination:\n\nInspection: There is a visible deformity of the right ankle with a small skin abrasion over the lateral malleolus. There is significant swelling and erythema around the ankle joint.\n\nPalpation: There is tenderness to palpation over the lateral malleolus, along the anterior and posterior tibiofibular ligaments, and over the lateral ligaments (ATFL, CFL, PTFL). The ankle is tender to deep pressure.\n\nRange of Motion: There is a painful and limited range of motion in the ankle joint, particularly with dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n\nStrength: There is weakness in ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion.\n\nNeurological: There is intact sensation and motor function of the foot and ankle.\n\nSpecial Tests:\n\n*   **Ottawa Ankle Rules:** The patient meets the criteria for Ottawa Ankle Rules, indicating a high likelihood of fracture.\n\nImaging:\n\nX-rays of the right ankle were performed, showing a fracture of the distal fibula (lateral malleolus). The fracture is nondisplaced and oblique.\n\nDiagnosis:\n\nBased on the clinical presentation and X-ray findings, the patient is diagnosed with a right ankle sprain, specifically a fracture of the distal fibula.\n\nTreatment:\n\nThe patient was treated with immobilization in a cast for 6 weeks. He was instructed to keep his foot elevated and use crutches for ambulation. He was also prescribed a home exercise program including range of motion exercises, strengthening exercises for the quadriceps and hamstrings, and eccentric strengthening exercises for the ankle.\n\nOutcome:\n\nThe patient's cast was removed after 6 weeks. He was able to ambulate with minimal pain and swelling. He was discharged from physiotherapy with a home exercise program and instructed on proper footwear. He was scheduled for follow-up appointments to monitor his progress.\n\nOutcome:\n\nThe patient's cast was removed after 6 weeks. He was able to ambulate with minimal pain and swelling. He was discharged from physiotherapy with a home exercise program and instructed on proper footwear. He was scheduled for follow-up appointments to monitor his progress.", "rouge1": 0.407673860911271, "rouge2": 0.08166533226581266, "rougeL": 0.17585931254996004, "bert_precision": 0.7978804111480713, "bert_recall": 0.7875459790229797, "bert_f1": 0.792679488658905, "gen_words": 431, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.25, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.25, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.25, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.25, "judge_overall": 0.25, "judge_unsafe": 1.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "The treatment plan prescribes range of motion exercises for the ankle while the patient is immobilized in a cast for a distal fibula fracture, which is clinically inappropriate and potentially harmful. Additionally, prescribing quadriceps and hamstrings strengthening exercises is irrelevant and coul", "judge_reasoning": "The case study has several significant issues. First, the diagnosis is contradictory: it labels the condition as an 'ankle sprain' but the imaging confirms a distal fibula fracture - these are distinct diagnoses. The treatment plan is partially appropriate for a fracture (cast, crutches, elevation) but the home exercise program during immobilization (ROM, strengthening) is clinically inappropriate and potentially unsafe while in a cast. Quadriceps/hamstrings exercises are irrelevant to an ankle injury. The outcome section is duplicated verbatim. The Ottawa Ankle Rules result correctly prompted", "clinical_msk_score": 0.3511, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 126, "title": "Osteoporosis & Fracture", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Osteoporosis and Fracture Case Study", "rouge1": 0.007428040854224698, "rouge2": 0.0, "rougeL": 0.005571030640668523, "bert_precision": 0.829543948173523, "bert_recall": 0.6167997121810913, "bert_f1": 0.7075254917144775, "gen_words": 5, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.0, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.0, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.0, "judge_patient_safety": 0.0, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.0, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.0, "judge_overall": 0.02, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study provided contains only a title 'Osteoporosis and Fracture Case Study' with no actual clinical content. There is no patient presentation, subjective or objective assessment, diagnostic reasoning, differential diagnosis, special tests, treatment plan, dosage, progression, red flags, or safety considerations. The submission is essentially empty, making it impossible to evaluate clinical coherence, diagnostic accuracy, treatment completeness, patient safety, or clinical relevance. All criteria must score at the lowest level due to complete absence of content. There is no unsafe cont", "clinical_msk_score": 0.1491, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 133, "title": "Calf muscle tear", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Calf Muscle Tear\n\nIntroduction\n\nA 26-year-old male, a professional soccer player, presented to the physiotherapy clinic with acute onset of right lower leg pain and swelling following a collision during a training session. He was unable to bear weight on his right leg and complained of a \"pop\" sensation at the time of injury. Examination revealed a significant calf muscle tear, with tenderness and ecchymosis extending from the gastrocnemius to the soleus muscle.\n\nExamination Findings\n\nThe patient was unable to bear weight on the affected leg and presented with significant pain and swelling. There was a palpable defect along the posterior aspect of the lower leg, with tenderness along the tear line. The calf muscles were markedly swollen and tender to palpation. There was also ecchymosis extending from the site of the tear.\n\nImaging\n\nAn MRI scan was performed to assess the extent of the tear and rule out any associated injuries. The MRI confirmed a complete tear of the gastrocnemius muscle, with partial tears of the soleus muscle. The tear was located approximately 5cm proximal to the Achilles tendon insertion.\n\nTreatment Plan\n\nThe treatment plan aimed to restore pain-free function and return the patient to their sport. The initial phase involved reducing pain and swelling, followed by progressive strengthening and stretching exercises.\n\nPhase 1: Acute Phase (First 2 weeks)\n\nThe goal was to reduce pain and swelling. The patient was treated with ice packs, compression garments, and elevation. Pain relief was achieved with NSAIDs. Gentle range of motion exercises were performed to prevent stiffness.\n\nPhase 2: Subacute Phase (Weeks 3-6)\n\nOnce the pain and swelling subsided, the patient began active range of motion exercises. Strengthening exercises were introduced, starting with isometric contractions and gradually progressing to isotonic exercises. Stretching exercises were also included to improve flexibility.\n\nPhase 3: Rehabilitation Phase (Weeks 7-12)\n\nThe patient continued with strengthening and stretching exercises, gradually increasing the intensity and duration. Balance and proprioception exercises were added to improve neuromuscular control. Gait training was also implemented to restore normal walking patterns.\n\nPhase 4: Return to Sport Training (Weeks 13-16)\n\nThe patient returned to training with his team, gradually increasing the intensity and duration of his sessions. He was closely monitored to ensure he was not experiencing any pain or discomfort.\n\nOutcome\n\nThe patient was able to return to play within 8 weeks of his injury. He demonstrated significant improvement in strength, flexibility, and balance, and was able to perform at a high level during training and competition.", "rouge1": 0.3850931677018633, "rouge2": 0.06846473029045642, "rougeL": 0.18633540372670807, "bert_precision": 0.7737201452255249, "bert_recall": 0.7633767127990723, "bert_f1": 0.7685136198997498, "gen_words": 415, "judge_clinical_coherence": 0.5, "judge_diagnostic_accuracy": 0.25, "judge_treatment_completeness": 0.25, "judge_patient_safety": 0.5, "judge_clinical_relevance": 0.5, "judge_criteria_mean": 0.4, "judge_overall": 0.48, "judge_unsafe": 0.0, "judge_bias": 0.0, "judge_unsafe_reason": "", "judge_reasoning": "The case study presents a reasonable clinical narrative for a calf muscle tear in a professional soccer player. Strengths include logical phased rehabilitation, appropriate imaging (MRI), and relevant interventions. However, several gaps exist: no formal special tests documented (Thompson test, single-leg heel raise), no differential diagnosis discussed, no specific dosage/frequency/sets/reps for exercises, no mention of red flags (DVT risk given significant swelling/ecchymosis), and the outcome is inconsistent - the plan runs 16 weeks but the patient returned to play at 8 weeks, which contrad", "clinical_msk_score": 0.4803, "judge_tag": "compact:claude-sonnet-4-6"}
{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 140, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Clubfoot\n\nClient: A 2-year-old infant\n\nHistory:\n\nChief Complaint: Right foot deformity, rigid, with a prominent dorsal and plantar calcaneus.\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n\nThe infant's mother noticed the foot deformity during a routine check-up at 6 months of age. The right foot was rigid and pointed downwards (plantarflexed) with a prominent bony bump on the top (dorsal) of the foot. The left foot was normal. The mother was concerned about the appearance of the foot and worried about the infant's future mobility.\n\nPast Medical History:\n\nUnremarkable\n\nSocial History:\n\nThe family has no known history of foot abnormalities. The mother works from home and is able to provide frequent home care.\n\nPhysical Examination:\n\nGeneral:\n\nThe infant is in no acute distress.\n\nRight Foot:\n\nThe right foot was in a rigid plantarflexed position. The right calcaneus was prominent and bony. The right foot was held in a neutral position by the mother.\n\nLeft Foot:\n\nNormal\n\nGait:\n\nThe infant ambulated with a wide-based gait, favoring the left leg.\n\nNeurological:\n\nNormal\n\nImaging:\n\nX-rays of the right foot were obtained, showing a calcaneal varus deformity with a calcaneal spur.\n\nAssessment:\n\nThe infant has a rigid right clubfoot with a calcaneal varus deformity.\n\nPlan:\n\n1.  Observation: Continue to observe the infant's right foot.\n\n2.  Home Care: Continue with home care.\n\n3.  Physical Therapy: Continue with physical therapy.\n\n4.  Medication: Continue with medication.\n\n5.  Surgical: Continue with surgery.\n\nOutcome:\n\nThe infant was treated with physical therapy and surgery. The infant was treated with physical therapy and surgery. The infant was treated with physical therapy and surgery. The infant was treated with physical therapy and surgery. The infant was treated with physical the