{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 141, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Case Study: Clubfoot in a 1-Year-Old Infant\n\nClient: A 1-year-old infant female with bilateral clubfoot.\n\nHistory:\n\nThe infant was born to a 25-year-old mother who was 2 weeks pregnant when she was diagnosed with an autoimmune condition that can cause swelling in the extremities. The infant was born at 38 weeks gestation with a birth weight of 3.2 kg. The mother was advised to keep the infant's legs straight at all times and to not walk on the infant's feet. The infant was assessed by a paediatric orthopaedic surgeon, who confirmed the diagnosis of bilateral clubfoot.\n\nClinical Examination:\n\nThe infant was seen in the clinic for the first time at 1 month of age. Her feet were held in equinus and varus positions. The infant's legs were also held in a dorsiflexed position. The infant's gait was normal, but she had difficulty walking on her toes. Her mother was very concerned about the infant's condition and wanted to know if there was any treatment available. The mother was advised to keep the infant's feet straight at all times and to not walk on the infant's feet.\n\nThe infant was referred to a paediatric orthopaedic surgeon for further evaluation. The orthopaedic surgeon assessed the infant and determined that she had bilateral clubfoot. The orthopaedic surgeon advised the mother to keep the infant's feet straight at all times and to not walk on the infant's feet. The orthopaedic surgeon also advised the mother to keep the infant's feet straight at all times and to not walk on the infant's feet.\n\nIntervention:\n\nThe infant was treated with a combination of physical therapy and orthotics. The physical therapy included stretching exercises and strengthening exercises. The orthotics were designed to keep the infant's feet straight.\n\nThe infant was also treated with casting. The casting was done in the morning and remov