{"model_pair": "medgemma3", "name": "A0_tuned_teacher", "seed": 0, "case_id": 143, "title": "Clubfoot", "sample_idx": 0, "generation": "Clubfoot is a congenital deformity of the foot in which the sole of the foot is turned inward, towards the thigh, and the foot is adducted (pulled towards the midline). It is one of the most common congenital foot deformities and can be associated with other abnormalities, such as the absence of the fibular head (in syndactyly), the absence of the talus (in talipes equinovarus), or the absence of the lateral cuneiform and cuboid bones (in pre-tibial clubfoot).\n\nClubfoot is most commonly associated with oligohydramnios (low amniotic fluid) or oligohydramnios with polyhydramnios (high amniotic fluid), which can be caused by fetal abnormalities, maternal conditions, or placental problems. In some cases, clubfoot is caused by a combination of these factors.\n\nThe etiology of clubfoot is not fully understood, but it is believed to be related to abnormal development of the bones and ligaments of the foot. The exact mechanism of the deformity is not known, but it is thought to be caused by abnormal development of the foot bones and ligaments.\n\nThe diagnosis of clubfoot is typically made by examining the foot and assessing the position of the foot. A physical examination may include the use of a foot casting technique to determine the severity of the deformity.\n\nThe treatment of clubfoot is primarily aimed at correcting the deformity and restoring normal foot function. The main treatment is a series of serial casting procedures, followed by a period of immobilization in a brace. The goal is to correct the deformity and restore normal foot function.\n\nThe prognosis of clubfoot is generally good, especially when treated early. Most children with clubfoot will be able to walk independently within a few months of treatment.\n\nThe main treatment for clubfoot is a series of serial casting procedures, followed by a period of immobilizatio